1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,480
You know, usually when we talk about computer memory or even our own human memory, there's this expectation of a static, highly organized archiving system.

2
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:10,620
Right, like a filing cabinet.

3
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:25,800
Yeah, exactly. I always picture this massive, well-lit warehouse. You walk in, you put a box on a designated shelf, the inventory database logs the exact location, and then, you know, a year later when you need it, you just point at the shelf and say, there it is.

4
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:32,800
It is an incredibly comforting visualization. I mean, we gravitate toward the idea of a localized state.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:34,420
The localized state.

6
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:46,700
Yeah, a fixed snapshot of exactly how things are right now. We build our mental models and our physical machines, too, around the illusion that the current state of a system tells us everything we need to know about it.

7
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,620
Like box on the shelf or no box on the shelf.

8
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:48,780
Yeah.

9
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,060
Binary. Clean.

10
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,380
Exactly. Clean.

11
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:59,640
But then you step into the world of possibility space and trajectory-based computation, and, well, suddenly that pristine warehouse just burns to the ground.

12
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:00,800
Oh, absolutely ashes.

13
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:09,280
We are looking at a computational and cognitive landscape today that is an absolute inversion of how we normally perceive reality.

14
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:16,220
So, if you are joining us for this deep dive, you're likely looking for that intellectual aha moment, right?

15
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:21,160
The one that bridges your everyday habits with some seriously deep theoretical computer science.

16
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:27,400
Yes. And we have an incredibly dense, profoundly fascinating source to get us there today.

17
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:39,300
We do. The document on the table is a 96-page monograph. It's titled Sphere Pop, A Complete Theory, Histories, Continuations, and the Algebra of Reachable Computation.

18
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:40,380
That is quite the title.

19
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:50,740
Right. It is written by an independent researcher who goes by the name Flyxion. And, um, on the surface, it functions as a complete formal theory for a radically new programming language called Sphere Pop.

20
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:52,380
But it's not just a programming manual.

21
00:01:52,540 --> 00:02:03,860
Not at all. Beneath the syntax and all the theorems, it doubles as a sweeping philosophical treatise. It touches on epistemology, the nature of memory, and, like, the foundational infrastructure of human civilization.

22
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:12,340
So, the mission of this deep dive is to completely dismantle how we think about computer programming. And, by extension, our own memories.

23
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,380
That's the goal.

24
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:27,160
We are going to explore a radical, almost disorienting claim. The claim that history is the fundamental substance. And, you know, the way things are right now, that box on the shelf, is nothing more than a mathematical illusion.

25
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:35,880
We are going to fundamentally rethink what a simple, scribbled, grocery list has in common with the deepest mathematics of computation.

26
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,300
I love that. The core convergence of the paper is stated very early on.

27
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:40,540
Right.

28
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,260
It sets the tone for everything.

29
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:50,120
Yeah. The author writes something really striking. They say, a note is an externalized continuation. A Sphere Pop history is an internalized note.

30
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:59,600
Okay. Let's unpack that. Because, to understand how computers should work, according to the Sphere Pop architecture, we actually have to step away from the keyboard first.

31
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:04,000
We do. We have to look at how human beings externalize knowledge in the physical world.

32
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:05,800
Right. We have to talk about taking notes.

33
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:10,080
The paper starts by attacking something it calls the passive theory of notes.

34
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:18,640
Yes. So, the passive theory of notes is the default assumption most of us operate under. It posits that notes are essentially prosthetic memories.

35
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:20,660
Like a crutch for a leaky brain.

36
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:30,460
Exactly. They are viewed as passive records of the past, created specifically to compensate for the biological reality that our brains are incredibly forgetful.

37
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:30,740
Sure.

38
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:36,500
So, under this theory, a note's entire value is judged by its retrospective fidelity.

39
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:44,360
Retrospective fidelity, meaning, how accurately does this piece of paper reflect what just happened?

40
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:55,800
Precisely that. If you experience an event, say, an important lecture, and you write down what the speaker said, the passive theory argues that a good note perfectly reproduces the past event.

41
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,460
And a bad note distorts it, or leaves things out.

42
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:01,860
Right. The orientation of the artifact is entirely backward-looking.

43
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:03,380
Okay, but I have to push back here.

44
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:03,600
Yeah.

45
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,460
Because isn't that exactly what taking notes in a meeting actually is?

46
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:07,220
Tell me how you mean.

47
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:20,480
Well, if I am sitting in a boardroom, and my boss is listing off a dozen action items for the quarter, and I am desperately typing on my laptop so I don't forget what was just said, I am quite literally recording the past.

48
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:22,100
It definitely feels that way.

49
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:29,080
Right. My brain is going to forget those 12 items by lunch. My notes are the prosthetic memory of that meeting.

50
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:36,720
The author acknowledges that this retrospective illusion is really powerful, but Flyxion dismantles it by asking us to shift our gaze.

51
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:37,460
Shift it where?

52
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:44,920
Away from celebrated historical records or meeting minutes, and instead look at the most common, mundane notes we create.

53
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,640
Consider a standard grocery list.

54
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:50,000
Okay. Milk, eggs, bread.

55
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:53,840
Look at that list. Does it record anything that happened in the past?

56
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:56,720
Well, no. I mean, I haven't bought the eggs yet. I haven't even gone to the store.

57
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:06,260
Exactly. The items on that list do not exist in the form the list anticipates. They haven't been purchased. They haven't been placed in your cart. They haven't been carried into your kitchen.

58
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:08,440
Oh, wow. I see what you're saying.

59
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:13,300
The list is definitively not a memory. It is a structured record of a future act.

60
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:18,860
That is a very weird way to think about a piece of paper on my fridge.

61
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:25,700
The author provides a whole cascade of these examples. A blueprint describes a building that has not yet been constructed.

62
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:29,500
A musical playlist is a record of a future act of listening.

63
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:34,060
Source code itself records computations that have not yet been executed.

64
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,100
A calendar entry records a commitment you haven't yet honored.

65
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:41,900
None of these artifacts are memories in the passive retrospective sense.

66
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:42,380
Exactly.

67
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:47,380
So the passive theory gets the phenomenology of note-taking completely backwards.

68
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:54,120
When I write down a to-do list, I am not preserving the past at all. I am literally constructing a future.

69
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:59,600
Taking it a step further, the paper introduces a concept from theoretical computer science called a continuation.

70
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:01,000
A continuation. Okay.

71
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:06,440
If a state is a snapshot of right now, a continuation is the entire sequence of what comes next.

72
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,060
It is the future execution that is enabled by a present configuration.

73
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:13,980
And this leads to the paper's core thesis on human artifacts, right?

74
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:15,400
The continuation account of notes.

75
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:23,180
Yes. The formal definition given in the source is that a note is any artifact that increases the reachability of a continuation.

76
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:31,920
Reachability. Meaning it makes a specific future trajectory more probable or significantly less costly to traverse.

77
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:32,480
Right.

78
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:39,240
To measure this, Flyxion introduces two mathematical concepts that completely reframe how we value information.

79
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:39,700
Which are?

80
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:42,420
Continuation volume and reachability leverage.

81
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:48,380
Continuation volume refers to the sheer number of distinct futures a single note opens up.

82
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,600
Okay. Volume makes sense. What about the leverage part?

83
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:53,700
Reachability leverage is where the everyday practicality hits.

84
00:06:54,160 --> 00:07:01,980
Reachability leverage is the energetic or temporal cost you save by having the note versus the cost of figuring everything out from first principles without it.

85
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:03,900
The leverage aspect makes perfect sense.

86
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:09,880
Let's say I'm reading a massive, incredibly dense, 500-page textbook on organic chemistry.

87
00:07:10,140 --> 00:07:10,380
Okay.

88
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:17,320
And I come across a specific formula and I spend 10 seconds folding over the corner of the page and hitting a paragraph with a yellow highlighter.

89
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,340
Just a 10-second physical action.

90
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:19,920
Right.

91
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:25,560
The cost of creating that note, the physical act of dog, earing, and highlighting is negligible.

92
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:26,460
10 seconds.

93
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:39,120
But two years later, when I am in a lab and I desperately need that exact formula, that 10-second physical deformation of the paper saves me three hours of frantically flipping through 500 pages.

94
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:42,040
That is an insane amount of reachability leverage.

95
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:42,540
Yeah.

96
00:07:42,740 --> 00:07:45,660
And notice what gives that dog-eared page its value.

97
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:50,060
Its worth has absolutely nothing to do with how well it remembers the past act of you reading the book.

98
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:53,240
Right. Its entire value is derived from the future access it guarantees.

99
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:57,140
Flyxion argues that notes are actually anti-collapse artifacts.

100
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:59,900
Our biological memory is constantly collapsing.

101
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:03,080
The universe's entropy is constantly collapsing possibilities.

102
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:09,840
So notes are physical anchors we drop into reality to preserve access to futures that would otherwise be permanently lost.

103
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:10,860
Beautifully said.

104
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,080
Okay. This is a brilliant philosophical reframing.

105
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:15,060
But here is the massive disconnect.

106
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:24,260
If human beings organically build notes to preserve access to futures, why on earth do we build computers to only care about the present moment?

107
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:28,160
And this bridges us perfectly into the critique of traditional computation.

108
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:37,760
Right. Because if we look at the standard premise of programming, the way everyone is taught to code from day one, it's entirely state-centric.

109
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:44,180
You open any introductory textbook on Python or C++, and the very first lesson is about variables.

110
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:49,260
You are taught that a variable is a named location in memory that holds a value.

111
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,820
You write x equals 5. Then on the next line, you write y equals x plus 1.

112
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:59,000
It's the warehouse analogy again. I have a box labeled x, and I put a 5 inside it.

113
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:04,740
Right. It feels intuitive because it mirrors how we naively interact with physical objects.

114
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:08,680
A ball is red. Then we paint it blue. Its state changed.

115
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:11,280
Traditional computation operates on this paradigm.

116
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:17,400
A program starts in an initial state. A sequence of instructions mutates that state step by step.

117
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:22,280
And whatever the final state of the machine happens to be when the program halts, well, that's your answer.

118
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:25,160
But the author of SpherePop calls this the state illusion.

119
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:26,480
The state illusion.

120
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:30,960
Yes. And there's a specific foundational mathematical proposition here.

121
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:33,280
Proposition 2.2 in the text.

122
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:37,200
It claims that state is not a real physical property.

123
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,960
State is actually a degenerate projection that collapses and erases history.

124
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:43,960
Okay. We need to break down the math here.

125
00:09:44,260 --> 00:09:48,880
What exactly does it mean that state is a degenerate projection?

126
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:56,780
In mathematics, a function or a projection is injective if every distinct input maps to a unique distinct output.

127
00:09:57,220 --> 00:09:58,040
Nothing is lost.

128
00:09:58,260 --> 00:09:59,760
Okay. So a one-to-one mapping.

129
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:08,120
Exactly. A projection becomes non-injective or degenerate when it collapses multiple, completely different inputs into the exact same output.

130
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:09,440
Can you give me a visual for that?

131
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:10,780
Think of a shadow on a wall.

132
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:14,980
If you shine a light on a cylinder from the top down, it casts a circular shadow.

133
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:19,040
If you shine a light on a sphere, it also casts a circular shadow.

134
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:19,560
Oh, wow.

135
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:24,880
The 3D history of those objects is completely different, but the 2D state, the shadow, is identical.

136
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:29,660
The projection to a lower dimension threw away the vital context of what the object actually is.

137
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:34,600
So applied to computers, the history is the sequence of all events that have actually occurred in the system.

138
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:38,780
But the computer's RAM, its memory, is finite.

139
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:44,240
Right. The space of all possible histories in an active system is infinitely large.

140
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,960
Every action, every millisecond, adds a new branch to the tree of history.

141
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:55,960
But the space of observable states, the physical transistors holding bits in a computer's memory, is strictly finite.

142
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:03,500
Therefore, any mathematical mapping from the infinite space of history to the finite space of memory must be non-injective.

143
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:06,180
The system is forced to throw away the trajectory.

144
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:10,640
It keeps the summary, but it permanently burns the evidence of how it arrived at that summary.

145
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:13,420
That lost information is the degeneracy.

146
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:15,280
Why does this actually matter, though?

147
00:11:15,340 --> 00:11:20,100
I mean, as a programmer, if I just want to know that X is 5, why do I care how it became 5?

148
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:27,420
The paper argues that you care because this foundation of amnesia is the root cause of almost every catastrophic failure in modern software.

149
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:28,900
Really? Like what?

150
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:32,740
Think about the most notorious, difficult-to-track bugs in computer science.

151
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:34,300
Race conditions.

152
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:36,140
Use after free vulnerabilities.

153
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:37,200
Okay, yeah.

154
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:38,420
Those are a nightmare.

155
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:42,700
They do not happen because the final state is mathematically wrong.

156
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:50,540
They happen precisely because traditional languages conflate distinct histories into a single, indistinguishable state.

157
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,680
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

158
00:11:52,740 --> 00:11:53,960
Let's take a race condition.

159
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:54,540
Go for it.

160
00:11:54,540 --> 00:12:05,600
So, I have a banking app, and two different processes, Thread A and Thread B, are trying to access the same bank account variable at the exact same millisecond.

161
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:07,640
Right, a classic concurrency problem.

162
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:09,280
Thread A wants to deposit money.

163
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,080
Thread B wants to withdraw money.

164
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:13,600
Because they overlap, they overwrite each other.

165
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:17,620
When the dust settles, the final state of the account variable might be $100.

166
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:23,540
But the computer literally has no idea if Thread A wrote that $100 or if Thread B wrote it.

167
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:25,880
Or if it was some corrupted combination of both.

168
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:32,840
The state says $100, but the history of who did what and when have been permanently erased by the architecture of the language.

169
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:35,880
That is the exact mechanism of a race condition.

170
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:41,720
Or consider a use-after-free bug, which is how a massive percentage of security hacks occur.

171
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:42,980
How does that play out here?

172
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:48,180
A program creates a pointer, a reference to a specific location in memory.

173
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:52,160
Later, the program says, I'm done with this memory, free it up.

174
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:53,440
The memory is freed.

175
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:57,820
But due to a bug, the original pointer still exists in the system.

176
00:12:58,100 --> 00:12:58,580
Exactly.

177
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,820
The state of that pointer hasn't changed.

178
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:03,620
It still holds the exact same memory address.

179
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:10,760
But the history of that memory region, the crucial fact that a free event occurred, is not attached to the pointer's state.

180
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:12,260
The system forgot the trajectory.

181
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:19,800
So the program follows the pointer, accesses memory it no longer owns, and the system crashes or gets hijacked by a malicious payload.

182
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:24,400
I am thinking about how developers actually deal with this in the real world.

183
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:27,780
And it is honestly wild when you frame it this way.

184
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:32,000
It's the difference between a conventional software debugger and a git commit history.

185
00:13:32,140 --> 00:13:34,120
Oh, that is a fantastic comparison.

186
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:43,140
If my Python code crashes and I open a traditional debugger, I am basically stepping into the role of a forensic archaeologist arriving at a crime scene.

187
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:53,060
I am looking at a crash state, a dead body on the floor, and I am desperately trying to reverse engineer a timeline of events based on scattered clues.

188
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:58,920
Because the actual evidence of the timeline was systematically destroyed by the computer as it was executing.

189
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:02,700
A debugger is a tool designed to mitigate the tragedy of state erasure.

190
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:07,560
You are trying to guess the history that the machine was explicitly designed to forget.

191
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:12,060
But if I look at a version control system like Git, the philosophy is completely different.

192
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:16,300
A Git repository isn't just the current file tree sitting on my hard drive.

193
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:19,820
No, Git proves that the history is the actual substance.

194
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,800
And the current file tree is just a temporary, degenerate summary.

195
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:26,520
In Git, I don't have to guess how a bug got introduced.

196
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:30,820
I could see every single branch, every line of code added or deleted,

197
00:14:31,180 --> 00:14:36,740
every commit with a timestamp and an author stretching all the way back to the very first day the project started.

198
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:40,700
And this is the exact revelation that birthed the SpherePop architecture.

199
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:46,080
If history is what gets lost in traditional computing, causing structural fragility,

200
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:49,840
then history must become the primary substance of the machine.

201
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:53,120
SpherePop refuses to discard the trajectory.

202
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:59,180
The author points out that the state illusion in computing and the passive theory of notes in human cognition

203
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:02,380
are actually the exact same philosophical mistake.

204
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:02,860
How so?

205
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:08,260
Both identify value with a terminal state, the final biological memory,

206
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:12,940
or the final variable value rather than respecting the trajectory that produced it.

207
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:15,080
Okay, I am fully bought into the problem.

208
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:15,440
Yes.

209
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:18,840
State is an illusion that causes amnesia and crashes.

210
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:21,320
History is the truth.

211
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:22,420
Glad you're on board.

212
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:27,880
So practically speaking, how do you actually build a functioning programming language out of history?

213
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:32,620
I mean, if I can't use variables, if I can't just say, store this number here, what am I even doing?

214
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:37,420
This brings us to the theoretical core of the paper, the four operators of possibility.

215
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:38,240
The four operators.

216
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:38,600
Okay.

217
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:42,780
If you strip away the concept of a variable and you strip away the concept of a state,

218
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:45,100
what is the fundamental fabric you are left with?

219
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:49,140
According to SpherePop, you are lumped with possibility space,

220
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:51,940
the infinite landscape of all potential futures.

221
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:55,940
The author proposes that you do not need variables or memory addresses.

222
00:15:55,940 --> 00:16:00,820
You only need a minimal, mathematically complete set of four operations

223
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:03,940
to navigate and shape this possibility space.

224
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:09,620
And these four operations are entirely sufficient to build any conceivable computer program.

225
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:15,400
The four operators are pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.

226
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:17,200
We need to go through these meticulously.

227
00:16:17,540 --> 00:16:18,380
Let's start with pop.

228
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:20,100
What is the pop operator?

229
00:16:20,280 --> 00:16:21,960
Pop is the operator of commitment.

230
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:24,720
Its function is to narrow possibility.

231
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,060
Like making a choice.

232
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:26,580
Exactly.

233
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,240
Imagine you are standing at a crossroads with five distinct paths.

234
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:34,660
Pop is the singular act of committing to one specific path

235
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:37,620
and irreversibly removing it from the space of options.

236
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:39,700
It is the primitive act of moving forward in time.

237
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:40,060
Yes.

238
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:42,080
When you pop a value or an action,

239
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:44,980
you are effectively foreclosing all other alternate futures

240
00:16:44,980 --> 00:16:46,320
in which your choice was different.

241
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:47,760
So pop is taking a step.

242
00:16:47,980 --> 00:16:49,160
It's executing a choice.

243
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:49,600
Got it.

244
00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:51,000
The second operator is refuse.

245
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:53,960
The paper calls this documented inadmissibility.

246
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:57,300
This operator is arguably the most crucial invention in the architecture.

247
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:01,740
Refuse is how the system records that a specific path in the possibility space

248
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:03,720
was found to be invalid or dangerous.

249
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:07,560
But, and this is the vital distinction from traditional error handling,

250
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:11,640
the refuse operator always requires a reason, right?

251
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:12,220
Always.

252
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:14,820
It doesn't just block a path with a generic no.

253
00:17:15,140 --> 00:17:18,640
It requires a formal proof of no because of X.

254
00:17:18,840 --> 00:17:19,120
Okay.

255
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:22,880
Furthermore, unlike pop, which consumes a choice,

256
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:26,340
refuse does not remove the option from the structural space.

257
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:28,360
It leaves the path visible,

258
00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:32,880
but marks it as semantically inadmissible for any future trajectory.

259
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:36,240
I want to really ground why the reason is so important.

260
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:40,060
Why can't the system just hit a dead end, throw a red flag, and bounce back?

261
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:42,700
Because without a permanent attached reason,

262
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:46,240
a refused path is completely indistinguishable from a path

263
00:17:46,240 --> 00:17:48,640
that was simply never considered in the first place.

264
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:48,940
Ah.

265
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:51,380
The reason is the evidence of the exploration.

266
00:17:51,680 --> 00:17:54,820
The reason becomes a first-class component of the system's history.

267
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:56,120
Let me try an analogy.

268
00:17:56,680 --> 00:17:58,380
Let's say we were exploring a labyrinth.

269
00:17:58,820 --> 00:18:00,600
If I walk down a corridor and simply tell you,

270
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:02,340
don't open the blue door at the end,

271
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:03,380
you might listen to me today.

272
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:03,700
Sure.

273
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:04,680
But a week later,

274
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:06,980
when you are stressed and looking for an exit,

275
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:07,540
you might think,

276
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,160
well, maybe he just didn't like the color blue,

277
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:10,020
and you open it anyway.

278
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:11,020
Human nature.

279
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:12,880
But if I use the refuse operator,

280
00:18:13,460 --> 00:18:14,360
I am saying,

281
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:15,900
don't open the blue door,

282
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:18,220
and here is the irrefutable proof

283
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:20,460
that there is a literal tiger behind it.

284
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:21,520
That changes things.

285
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:21,840
Right.

286
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:25,220
That attached reason fundamentally and permanently

287
00:18:25,220 --> 00:18:27,760
alters your future relationship with that door.

288
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:29,880
It's not just a blocked path.

289
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:31,940
It is documented knowledge.

290
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:33,920
A perfect translation of the concept.

291
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:36,800
The third operator in the set is bind.

292
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:40,720
The paper defines this as dependency declaration.

293
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:42,140
What are we binding together?

294
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:45,820
Bind couples two independent trajectory elements together

295
00:18:45,820 --> 00:18:47,560
without consuming either of them.

296
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:48,980
It records a relationship.

297
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:49,500
Okay.

298
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:51,040
If pop is taking a step

299
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:52,940
and refuse is documenting a tiger,

300
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:55,280
bind is drawing a line on a map

301
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:56,700
connecting your current location

302
00:18:56,700 --> 00:18:58,020
to the location of the tiger.

303
00:18:58,640 --> 00:18:58,960
It says,

304
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:00,340
the existence of this element

305
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:01,700
is now formally dependent

306
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:03,160
on the existence of that element.

307
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:05,080
And finally, we have the fourth operator.

308
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:05,340
Go.

309
00:19:05,420 --> 00:19:05,860
Collapse.

310
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:08,280
The paper calls this observation under rule.

311
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:10,420
Collapse is essentially a meta operation.

312
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:12,340
Pop, refuse, and bind

313
00:19:12,340 --> 00:19:13,840
all operate silently

314
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:14,640
on the invisible,

315
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:16,600
ever-growing history of the system.

316
00:19:16,760 --> 00:19:18,200
But we eventually need to see something.

317
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:18,800
Right.

318
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:20,200
Collapse is the mechanism

319
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:22,060
that makes those invisible histories

320
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:23,160
visible to an observer.

321
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:26,580
It maps the vast, complex history space

322
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:29,060
down to a finite, observable state.

323
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:30,200
But it does so safely.

324
00:19:30,560 --> 00:19:31,460
It acts as a lens.

325
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:32,740
And, crucially,

326
00:19:33,140 --> 00:19:34,740
a collapse operation requires

327
00:19:34,740 --> 00:19:36,200
an admissibility certificate.

328
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,940
A proof that the history

329
00:19:37,940 --> 00:19:38,780
it is summarizing

330
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:39,780
is actually valid

331
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,200
and hasn't violated

332
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:42,620
any refuse documents

333
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:43,240
along the way.

334
00:19:43,700 --> 00:19:44,480
I have to pause here.

335
00:19:44,700 --> 00:19:44,880
I mean,

336
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:46,020
I have written

337
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:47,160
my fair share of code.

338
00:19:47,700 --> 00:19:48,140
Python,

339
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:48,960
JavaScript,

340
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:49,860
a little C.

341
00:19:50,360 --> 00:19:51,160
So you know

342
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:52,040
the standard toolkit.

343
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:52,480
Yeah.

344
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:53,520
And I am looking at these

345
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:55,640
four highly abstract,

346
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:57,520
almost philosophical verbs.

347
00:19:57,720 --> 00:19:58,060
Commit,

348
00:19:58,420 --> 00:19:58,880
refuse,

349
00:19:59,260 --> 00:19:59,600
bind,

350
00:19:59,980 --> 00:20:00,300
observe.

351
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:01,740
Are you seriously telling me

352
00:20:01,740 --> 00:20:02,440
that I can build

353
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:04,300
any standard computer program

354
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:05,060
with just this?

355
00:20:05,400 --> 00:20:05,800
I am.

356
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:06,920
I can build an if statement.

357
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:07,960
I can manage RAM.

358
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:09,640
I can handle network concurrency

359
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:11,340
with just these four things.

360
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:12,240
The paper dedicates

361
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:13,380
a massive section,

362
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:14,600
theorem 3.7,

363
00:20:14,660 --> 00:20:15,180
the completeness

364
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:16,000
of the four operators,

365
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:17,200
to proving exactly that.

366
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:18,360
Everything you do

367
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:19,340
in a traditional language

368
00:20:19,340 --> 00:20:20,140
is just a macro

369
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:20,900
built on top

370
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:21,760
of these four primitives.

371
00:20:21,860 --> 00:20:22,540
Okay, prove it.

372
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:23,820
Let's take the most basic example

373
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:24,500
I brought up earlier.

374
00:20:24,840 --> 00:20:26,500
Variable assignment dot x equals 5.

375
00:20:26,820 --> 00:20:27,060
Right.

376
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:29,040
How do you do x equals 5

377
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:30,000
without a variable?

378
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:30,460
Yeah.

379
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:31,480
In SpherePop,

380
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:33,120
variable assignment doesn't exist.

381
00:20:33,620 --> 00:20:33,920
Instead,

382
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:35,360
the system executes

383
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:36,820
a pop of the value 5,

384
00:20:37,080 --> 00:20:38,100
committing the number 5

385
00:20:38,100 --> 00:20:38,860
into the history.

386
00:20:39,060 --> 00:20:39,760
Okay, so the 5

387
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:40,520
is a step we took.

388
00:20:40,620 --> 00:20:40,920
Then,

389
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:42,040
it executes a bind,

390
00:20:42,220 --> 00:20:44,280
coupling the human-readable symbol x

391
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:46,520
to that specific historical pop event.

392
00:20:46,960 --> 00:20:48,620
The value isn't stored in x.

393
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:50,340
x is just a pointer

394
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:51,580
to a moment in history

395
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:52,700
where 5 was chosen.

396
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:53,240
Wow.

397
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:53,600
Okay.

398
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:55,000
What about control flow?

399
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:56,720
How do I write an if statement?

400
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:57,100
Mm-hmm.

401
00:20:57,100 --> 00:20:58,280
Like, if the weather is raining,

402
00:20:58,640 --> 00:20:59,180
take an umbrella.

403
00:20:59,580 --> 00:20:59,820
Otherwise,

404
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:00,920
take sunglasses.

405
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:02,120
An if statement

406
00:21:02,120 --> 00:21:03,240
is a bifurcation

407
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:04,700
of possibility space.

408
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:06,100
The system evaluates

409
00:21:06,100 --> 00:21:06,600
the condition.

410
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:07,520
Is it raining?

411
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:09,540
That evaluation is a pop.

412
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:11,040
Let's say it is raining.

413
00:21:11,180 --> 00:21:11,380
Then,

414
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:12,240
the system pops

415
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:13,120
the true branch

416
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:14,140
taking the umbrella.

417
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:14,940
But what about

418
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:15,880
the false branch?

419
00:21:16,120 --> 00:21:17,080
In traditional code,

420
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:17,780
the false branch

421
00:21:17,780 --> 00:21:18,820
is just ignored.

422
00:21:19,260 --> 00:21:19,900
In SpherePop,

423
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:20,820
the system uses

424
00:21:20,820 --> 00:21:21,960
the refuse operator

425
00:21:21,960 --> 00:21:22,920
on the false branch,

426
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,060
permanently documenting

427
00:21:24,060 --> 00:21:25,000
exactly why

428
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,180
the sunglasses trajectory

429
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:27,080
was not taken,

430
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:28,520
because the rain condition

431
00:21:28,520 --> 00:21:29,820
evaluated to true.

432
00:21:30,180 --> 00:21:30,720
So the history

433
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:32,340
explicitly records

434
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:33,520
both the path taken

435
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:34,580
and the mathematically

436
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:35,580
certified reason

437
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:36,460
the alternate path

438
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:37,020
was rejected.

439
00:21:37,380 --> 00:21:37,860
Exactly.

440
00:21:38,020 --> 00:21:38,900
That is mind-blowing.

441
00:21:39,220 --> 00:21:40,240
The code doesn't just

442
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:41,180
know what it did,

443
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:42,460
it knows exactly why

444
00:21:42,460 --> 00:21:43,660
it didn't do something else.

445
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:43,960
Right.

446
00:21:44,120 --> 00:21:45,360
What about when things go wrong?

447
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:46,160
What about exceptions

448
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:47,360
or system crashes?

449
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:48,920
An exception being thrown

450
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:49,700
is no longer

451
00:21:49,700 --> 00:21:50,680
a catastrophic event

452
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:52,020
that destroys the call stack.

453
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:53,480
An exception is simply

454
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:54,500
a refuse operator

455
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:55,640
that carries its reason,

456
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:57,580
perhaps a file-not-found error

457
00:21:57,580 --> 00:21:58,880
as a first-class certificate.

458
00:21:59,240 --> 00:22:00,220
The system doesn't crash.

459
00:22:00,740 --> 00:22:01,520
It just adds

460
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:03,120
a highly detailed refusal

461
00:22:03,120 --> 00:22:03,900
to the history.

462
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:04,480
Yes.

463
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:05,880
And memory management,

464
00:22:06,760 --> 00:22:07,480
freeing up RAM.

465
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:09,100
Even memory deallocation

466
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:10,440
is just a refuse operator

467
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:11,700
applied to a memory pointer.

468
00:22:12,140 --> 00:22:13,820
It records that the memory region

469
00:22:13,820 --> 00:22:15,040
is no longer admissible

470
00:22:15,040 --> 00:22:15,580
to access.

471
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:16,920
So if a later part

472
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:17,420
of the program

473
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:18,820
tries to access that memory?

474
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:19,580
Which would cause

475
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:20,900
a use-after-free bug

476
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:21,540
in C++.

477
00:22:21,960 --> 00:22:22,160
Right.

478
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:23,440
SpherePop simply traces

479
00:22:23,440 --> 00:22:24,020
the history,

480
00:22:24,560 --> 00:22:25,320
hits the refuse

481
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:25,860
documentation,

482
00:22:26,520 --> 00:22:27,060
and stops.

483
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:28,840
It turns a silent,

484
00:22:29,080 --> 00:22:30,200
dangerous memory corruption

485
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:31,140
into a clean,

486
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:32,460
mathematically proven

487
00:22:32,460 --> 00:22:33,460
type-level error.

488
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:34,900
The history protects

489
00:22:34,900 --> 00:22:35,400
the future.

490
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:36,820
The elegance of that

491
00:22:36,820 --> 00:22:37,800
is hard to overstate.

492
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:39,920
And out of this architecture,

493
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:41,040
Fitchin derives

494
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:41,740
this overarching

495
00:22:41,740 --> 00:22:42,880
mathematical principle

496
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:44,220
called the conservation

497
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:45,600
law of possibility.

498
00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:47,140
Theorem 3.8.

499
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:48,300
It reads like a law

500
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:49,100
of thermodynamics

501
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:49,900
for computation.

502
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:51,400
It mathematically proves

503
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:52,620
that in this architecture,

504
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:54,640
possibility cannot be created

505
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:55,240
or destroyed.

506
00:22:55,740 --> 00:22:56,900
It can only be consumed

507
00:22:56,900 --> 00:22:57,620
by a pop

508
00:22:57,620 --> 00:22:58,580
or documented

509
00:22:58,580 --> 00:22:59,360
by a refuse.

510
00:22:59,660 --> 00:23:01,140
Because every single operation,

511
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:02,120
whether it succeeds

512
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:02,700
or fails,

513
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:03,860
must be appended

514
00:23:03,860 --> 00:23:04,500
to the log.

515
00:23:04,980 --> 00:23:05,820
Every operation

516
00:23:05,820 --> 00:23:07,180
strictly increases

517
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:08,080
the overall length

518
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:09,300
of the system's history.

519
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:10,640
Which means time

520
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:11,700
in the SpherePop universe

521
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:13,140
is strictly irreversible.

522
00:23:13,700 --> 00:23:15,420
You can never truly hit undo

523
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:17,120
and return to a pristine

524
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:18,060
prior state.

525
00:23:18,180 --> 00:23:19,140
Because to go backwards,

526
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:20,920
to truly erase a mistake

527
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:22,220
would require erasing

528
00:23:22,220 --> 00:23:23,460
the history of the steps

529
00:23:23,460 --> 00:23:24,700
you took to make the mistake,

530
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:25,620
which violates

531
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:26,760
the foundational premise

532
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:28,100
of the entire framework.

533
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:29,120
In SpherePop,

534
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:30,820
computation isn't the manipulation

535
00:23:30,820 --> 00:23:31,980
of static values.

536
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:34,160
Computation is the progressive,

537
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:35,820
irreversible restriction

538
00:23:35,820 --> 00:23:36,600
of possibility.

539
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:37,440
Beautifully put.

540
00:23:37,600 --> 00:23:38,380
This is where the paper

541
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:39,240
pulls off a maneuver

542
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:40,680
that completely caught me off guard.

543
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:40,860
Yeah.

544
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:41,680
The taxonomy.

545
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:42,320
Yes.

546
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:44,400
Having spent 40 pages

547
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:45,300
establishing these

548
00:23:45,300 --> 00:23:46,640
four mathematical operators,

549
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:48,180
pop, refuse, bind,

550
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:49,780
collapse for machine computation,

551
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:51,920
Flyxion suddenly pans

552
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:52,840
the camera back out

553
00:23:52,840 --> 00:23:53,900
to human civilization.

554
00:23:54,540 --> 00:23:55,420
The author maps

555
00:23:55,420 --> 00:23:56,860
these exact same

556
00:23:56,860 --> 00:23:58,060
four operators

557
00:23:58,060 --> 00:23:59,720
onto the physical artifacts

558
00:23:59,720 --> 00:24:01,200
we create in society,

559
00:24:01,780 --> 00:24:02,840
generating what is called

560
00:24:02,840 --> 00:24:04,780
the SpherePop note taxonomy.

561
00:24:05,120 --> 00:24:06,500
It is a stunning convergence.

562
00:24:07,400 --> 00:24:08,160
The assertion is that

563
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:09,040
the artifacts we use

564
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:10,320
to organize human society

565
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:11,600
aren't just loosely

566
00:24:11,600 --> 00:24:12,940
analogous to computation.

567
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:14,700
They are literal realizations

568
00:24:14,700 --> 00:24:15,760
of the exact same

569
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:16,700
four operators.

570
00:24:17,200 --> 00:24:18,240
The paper identifies

571
00:24:18,240 --> 00:24:20,040
seven distinct classes of notes.

572
00:24:20,120 --> 00:24:21,140
Let's weave through these

573
00:24:21,140 --> 00:24:22,320
because it completely changes

574
00:24:22,320 --> 00:24:23,140
how you look at a desk

575
00:24:23,140 --> 00:24:23,680
full of paper.

576
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:25,420
The first class is capture notes.

577
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:26,540
Capture notes map

578
00:24:26,540 --> 00:24:28,120
directly to the refuse operator.

579
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:29,320
The primary examples

580
00:24:29,320 --> 00:24:30,180
are photographs,

581
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:31,000
audio recordings,

582
00:24:31,180 --> 00:24:32,060
or a scientist's

583
00:24:32,060 --> 00:24:33,200
raw laboratory notebook.

584
00:24:33,320 --> 00:24:33,920
What are they doing?

585
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:35,200
They are refusing the collapse

586
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:36,420
of a perceptual trajectory.

587
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:38,260
When you take a photograph

588
00:24:38,260 --> 00:24:39,060
of a sunset,

589
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:40,140
that photograph

590
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:41,560
is a physical artifact

591
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:42,080
asserting,

592
00:24:42,720 --> 00:24:43,540
I refuse to let

593
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:44,940
the infinite visual complexity

594
00:24:44,940 --> 00:24:45,960
of the specific moment

595
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:46,940
in history be collapsed

596
00:24:46,940 --> 00:24:47,780
into a vague,

597
00:24:48,140 --> 00:24:48,940
low-resolution

598
00:24:48,940 --> 00:24:50,040
biological memory.

599
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:51,020
They are anchors

600
00:24:51,020 --> 00:24:51,820
against the decay

601
00:24:51,820 --> 00:24:52,480
of perception.

602
00:24:52,820 --> 00:24:53,360
I love that.

603
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:55,360
The second class

604
00:24:55,360 --> 00:24:56,600
is perspective notes,

605
00:24:56,820 --> 00:24:57,340
which maps

606
00:24:57,340 --> 00:24:58,460
to the bind operator.

607
00:24:58,620 --> 00:24:59,680
We touched on these earlier.

608
00:24:59,960 --> 00:25:00,480
Calendars,

609
00:25:00,680 --> 00:25:01,760
architectural blueprints,

610
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:02,860
project roadmaps,

611
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:05,020
they bind a present state

612
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:06,020
to a future action.

613
00:25:06,220 --> 00:25:06,740
Importantly,

614
00:25:06,860 --> 00:25:07,740
a calendar appointment

615
00:25:07,740 --> 00:25:08,780
does not execute

616
00:25:08,780 --> 00:25:09,280
the meeting.

617
00:25:09,440 --> 00:25:09,680
Right.

618
00:25:09,820 --> 00:25:10,580
That execution

619
00:25:10,580 --> 00:25:11,340
would be a POC.

620
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:12,360
The calendar

621
00:25:12,360 --> 00:25:13,200
merely records

622
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:13,900
the dependency

623
00:25:13,900 --> 00:25:14,920
between today

624
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:15,540
and tomorrow.

625
00:25:15,540 --> 00:25:17,480
The paper points out

626
00:25:17,480 --> 00:25:18,240
that human-written

627
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:18,820
source code

628
00:25:18,820 --> 00:25:19,360
is actually

629
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:20,300
a perspective note.

630
00:25:20,820 --> 00:25:21,440
It binds

631
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:22,900
a future machine execution

632
00:25:22,900 --> 00:25:23,740
to a present

633
00:25:23,740 --> 00:25:24,880
human specification.

634
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:26,080
The third class

635
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:27,220
is repair notes.

636
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:28,460
These combine

637
00:25:28,460 --> 00:25:30,020
refuse and pop.

638
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:31,340
Troubleshooting guides

639
00:25:31,340 --> 00:25:32,340
and maintenance manuals

640
00:25:32,340 --> 00:25:33,320
are the best examples.

641
00:25:33,980 --> 00:25:34,600
A repair note

642
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:35,760
is a preemptive reduction

643
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:37,200
of reconstruction costs.

644
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:38,020
So it anticipates

645
00:25:38,020 --> 00:25:38,440
a failure.

646
00:25:38,600 --> 00:25:39,160
A refusal

647
00:25:39,160 --> 00:25:40,200
in the real world,

648
00:25:40,340 --> 00:25:41,300
like a printer jamming.

649
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:42,520
It acknowledges

650
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:43,100
that the system

651
00:25:43,100 --> 00:25:44,660
has hit an inadmissible state

652
00:25:44,660 --> 00:25:45,300
and it provides

653
00:25:45,300 --> 00:25:45,900
a stored,

654
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:46,860
certified sequence

655
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:47,460
of POPs.

656
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:48,320
Step one,

657
00:25:48,580 --> 00:25:49,100
open tray.

658
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:49,940
Step two,

659
00:25:50,260 --> 00:25:50,880
remove paper.

660
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:52,220
This navigates

661
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:52,800
the user back

662
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:54,020
to a functional trajectory.

663
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:54,780
The fourth

664
00:25:54,780 --> 00:25:55,780
is refusal notes.

665
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:56,660
This is the pure

666
00:25:56,660 --> 00:25:57,140
application

667
00:25:57,140 --> 00:25:58,260
of the refuse operator.

668
00:25:58,700 --> 00:25:59,800
Mathematical proofs,

669
00:26:00,100 --> 00:26:01,180
cryptographic checksums,

670
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:02,300
legal deeds,

671
00:26:02,660 --> 00:26:03,880
their entire function

672
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:04,580
is to prevent

673
00:26:04,580 --> 00:26:05,220
the collapse

674
00:26:05,220 --> 00:26:05,940
of a distinction.

675
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:07,200
Like a math proof.

676
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:07,700
Yes.

677
00:26:07,700 --> 00:26:08,920
A 50-page

678
00:26:08,920 --> 00:26:09,920
mathematical proof

679
00:26:09,920 --> 00:26:11,180
refuses the collapse

680
00:26:11,180 --> 00:26:11,980
of a complex

681
00:26:11,980 --> 00:26:13,080
logical trajectory

682
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:14,960
into a mere assertion.

683
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:16,580
You don't have to just trust

684
00:26:16,580 --> 00:26:17,580
that a theorem is true.

685
00:26:18,260 --> 00:26:19,180
The refusal note

686
00:26:19,180 --> 00:26:20,540
preserves the exact

687
00:26:20,540 --> 00:26:21,780
rigorous path

688
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:22,340
of truth,

689
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:23,520
preventing anyone

690
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:24,060
from taking

691
00:26:24,060 --> 00:26:24,960
an inadmissible

692
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:25,960
logical shortcut

693
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:27,080
whether or not

694
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:27,980
anyone ever reads

695
00:26:27,980 --> 00:26:28,940
the full proof again.

696
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:29,580
Fifth,

697
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:31,160
we have generative notes.

698
00:26:31,560 --> 00:26:32,220
These correspond

699
00:26:32,220 --> 00:26:33,300
to the POP operator.

700
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:34,300
Physical maps,

701
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:35,440
scientific theories,

702
00:26:35,600 --> 00:26:36,280
grammar rules,

703
00:26:36,420 --> 00:26:37,300
they open paths

704
00:26:37,300 --> 00:26:38,060
that were previously

705
00:26:38,060 --> 00:26:39,200
completely unreachable.

706
00:26:39,280 --> 00:26:40,480
A map doesn't just record

707
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:41,220
where someone walked?

708
00:26:41,300 --> 00:26:41,580
No,

709
00:26:41,580 --> 00:26:42,440
it creates access

710
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:43,680
to entirely new

711
00:26:43,680 --> 00:26:44,380
continuations

712
00:26:44,380 --> 00:26:45,540
in physical possibility

713
00:26:45,540 --> 00:26:46,540
space for anyone

714
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:47,260
holding them out.

715
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:47,640
Okay,

716
00:26:47,780 --> 00:26:48,820
now for the sixth class.

717
00:26:49,180 --> 00:26:49,740
This is the one

718
00:26:49,740 --> 00:26:50,600
I really wrestled with.

719
00:26:51,020 --> 00:26:51,660
Collapse notes.

720
00:26:52,140 --> 00:26:52,960
Mapping directly

721
00:26:52,960 --> 00:26:54,000
to the collapse operator.

722
00:26:54,160 --> 00:26:54,760
The examples

723
00:26:54,760 --> 00:26:55,880
Flyxion gives are

724
00:26:55,880 --> 00:26:56,820
indexes in the back

725
00:26:56,820 --> 00:26:57,260
of a book,

726
00:26:57,660 --> 00:26:58,900
file names on a computer,

727
00:26:59,260 --> 00:27:00,560
biological taxonomies,

728
00:27:00,680 --> 00:27:01,880
and even human words

729
00:27:01,880 --> 00:27:02,400
themselves.

730
00:27:02,700 --> 00:27:03,420
This brings us

731
00:27:03,420 --> 00:27:04,560
to the profound paradox

732
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:05,440
of the collapse note.

733
00:27:06,420 --> 00:27:07,440
Every other class

734
00:27:07,440 --> 00:27:08,540
of note we just discussed

735
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:09,140
is designed

736
00:27:09,140 --> 00:27:10,320
to preserve information,

737
00:27:10,580 --> 00:27:11,380
to preserve history,

738
00:27:11,380 --> 00:27:11,780
Right.

739
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:12,640
Collapse notes,

740
00:27:12,920 --> 00:27:13,940
mathematically speaking,

741
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:15,740
exist to destroy

742
00:27:15,740 --> 00:27:16,420
information.

743
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:18,560
They take a complex,

744
00:27:18,840 --> 00:27:19,500
rich history

745
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:21,300
and forcefully compress it

746
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:22,060
into a tiny,

747
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:23,220
degenerate representation.

748
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:24,600
Let's really dig

749
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:25,420
into this paradox

750
00:27:25,420 --> 00:27:26,580
because the idea

751
00:27:26,580 --> 00:27:27,780
that destroying information

752
00:27:27,780 --> 00:27:29,580
is a necessary social good

753
00:27:29,580 --> 00:27:31,180
is counterintuitive.

754
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:32,020
Very much so.

755
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:32,360
Right.

756
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:33,040
Let's say I write

757
00:27:33,040 --> 00:27:34,240
a 500-page biography

758
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:35,140
of Abraham Lincoln.

759
00:27:35,260 --> 00:27:35,500
Okay.

760
00:27:35,660 --> 00:27:36,280
And then I give it

761
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:36,980
the title Lincoln.

762
00:27:37,160 --> 00:27:37,400
Okay.

763
00:27:37,400 --> 00:27:38,560
By summarizing

764
00:27:38,560 --> 00:27:39,680
half a million words

765
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:40,280
into a single

766
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:41,160
seven-letter word,

767
00:27:41,500 --> 00:27:42,200
I have engaged

768
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:43,420
in a massive destruction

769
00:27:43,420 --> 00:27:44,080
of information.

770
00:27:44,320 --> 00:27:45,120
I have thrown away

771
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:47,380
99.99% of the nuance.

772
00:27:47,480 --> 00:27:48,640
But what Flyxion points out

773
00:27:48,640 --> 00:27:49,640
is that this violence,

774
00:27:49,800 --> 00:27:50,360
this destruction,

775
00:27:50,360 --> 00:27:51,820
is absolutely necessary

776
00:27:51,820 --> 00:27:52,900
to make the book reachable.

777
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:53,300
Exactly.

778
00:27:53,460 --> 00:27:54,320
Let me phrase it differently.

779
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:56,120
The author formalizes this

780
00:27:56,120 --> 00:27:57,440
in Theorem 13.2,

781
00:27:57,940 --> 00:27:58,500
the necessity

782
00:27:58,500 --> 00:27:59,420
of collapse notes.

783
00:28:00,100 --> 00:28:01,340
Imagine a massive

784
00:28:01,340 --> 00:28:02,340
national library

785
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:03,980
containing 10 million books.

786
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:04,360
Okay.

787
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:05,440
But none of the books

788
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:06,520
have titles on the spine.

789
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:07,460
None of them have

790
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:08,220
authors listed.

791
00:28:08,680 --> 00:28:10,320
And there is no card catalog.

792
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:11,580
Sounds like a nightmare.

793
00:28:11,820 --> 00:28:13,020
The library preserves

794
00:28:13,020 --> 00:28:14,560
100% of the information

795
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:15,120
perfectly.

796
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:16,860
No history is lost.

797
00:28:17,700 --> 00:28:18,700
But the search cost

798
00:28:18,700 --> 00:28:20,220
to find a specific paragraph

799
00:28:20,220 --> 00:28:21,560
on a specific topic

800
00:28:21,560 --> 00:28:23,620
is so astronomically high

801
00:28:23,620 --> 00:28:24,460
that the books

802
00:28:24,460 --> 00:28:25,860
are effectively unreachable.

803
00:28:26,240 --> 00:28:26,980
The information

804
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:28,080
might as well not exist.

805
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:29,460
So by destroying

806
00:28:29,460 --> 00:28:30,060
the nuance

807
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:31,080
of the book's contents

808
00:28:31,080 --> 00:28:32,540
and slapping a single

809
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:33,720
highly compressed

810
00:28:33,720 --> 00:28:34,480
summary title

811
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:35,140
on the spine,

812
00:28:35,260 --> 00:28:36,040
a collapse note,

813
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:37,220
you impose structure

814
00:28:37,220 --> 00:28:37,920
on chaos.

815
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:38,340
Yes.

816
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:39,540
Language itself

817
00:28:39,540 --> 00:28:40,320
operates this way.

818
00:28:40,900 --> 00:28:41,660
The spoken word

819
00:28:41,660 --> 00:28:43,240
tree is a collapse note.

820
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:43,980
How so?

821
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:45,000
When I say tree,

822
00:28:45,260 --> 00:28:46,080
I am compressing

823
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:47,040
millions of unique

824
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:48,840
complex perceptual trajectories

825
00:28:48,840 --> 00:28:49,920
of individual leaves,

826
00:28:50,300 --> 00:28:50,920
rough bark,

827
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:52,120
branching patterns,

828
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:53,100
and shifting light

829
00:28:53,100 --> 00:28:54,340
into one single

830
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:56,160
retrievable auditory token.

831
00:28:56,480 --> 00:28:57,580
Without collapse notes

832
00:28:57,580 --> 00:28:58,900
constantly destroying nuance

833
00:28:58,900 --> 00:29:00,380
to create manageable symbols,

834
00:29:00,800 --> 00:29:01,540
we wouldn't suffer

835
00:29:01,540 --> 00:29:02,580
from a lack of facts.

836
00:29:02,960 --> 00:29:03,420
We would suffer

837
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:05,200
from total navigational failure.

838
00:29:05,340 --> 00:29:06,180
We would be paralyzed

839
00:29:06,180 --> 00:29:07,580
by the infinite fidelity

840
00:29:07,580 --> 00:29:08,280
of the universe.

841
00:29:08,440 --> 00:29:09,280
Which leads perfectly

842
00:29:09,280 --> 00:29:10,500
into the seventh aspect

843
00:29:10,500 --> 00:29:11,540
of this taxonomy.

844
00:29:11,860 --> 00:29:12,660
It's presented more

845
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:13,260
as a theorem

846
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:14,500
than a distinct class

847
00:29:14,500 --> 00:29:14,880
of notes,

848
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:16,100
but it completely

849
00:29:16,100 --> 00:29:17,320
shattered my perspective.

850
00:29:17,740 --> 00:29:18,940
The ephemerality inversion.

851
00:29:19,560 --> 00:29:21,160
Theorem 13.5.

852
00:29:21,160 --> 00:29:23,320
It is a profound realization

853
00:29:23,320 --> 00:29:25,380
about how we evaluate history.

854
00:29:26,060 --> 00:29:26,520
Traditionally,

855
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:27,700
in archaeology,

856
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:28,440
in literature,

857
00:29:28,740 --> 00:29:29,480
in history,

858
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:31,860
we inherently value notes

859
00:29:31,860 --> 00:29:32,540
that survive

860
00:29:32,540 --> 00:29:33,500
the test of time.

861
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:35,040
Ancient Roman manuscripts,

862
00:29:35,820 --> 00:29:37,120
undelivered medieval letters,

863
00:29:37,740 --> 00:29:38,720
the Dead Sea Scrolls.

864
00:29:38,760 --> 00:29:40,480
We view their physical survival

865
00:29:40,480 --> 00:29:41,480
across millennia

866
00:29:41,480 --> 00:29:42,840
as the ultimate triumph

867
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:43,900
of information.

868
00:29:44,380 --> 00:29:45,600
But the sphere pop theory

869
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:46,460
of continuations

870
00:29:46,460 --> 00:29:47,920
argues the exact opposite.

871
00:29:48,420 --> 00:29:49,420
It argues that

872
00:29:49,420 --> 00:29:50,440
if a note's continuation

873
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:51,620
is fully completed,

874
00:29:52,060 --> 00:29:53,260
its physical destruction

875
00:29:53,260 --> 00:29:54,560
actually represents

876
00:29:54,560 --> 00:29:55,640
optimal success.

877
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:57,480
Think back to the grocery list

878
00:29:57,480 --> 00:29:58,000
we discussed

879
00:29:58,000 --> 00:29:58,740
at the very beginning.

880
00:29:59,220 --> 00:29:59,940
You write the list

881
00:29:59,940 --> 00:30:00,600
on your fridge.

882
00:30:00,920 --> 00:30:01,840
You drive to the store.

883
00:30:02,320 --> 00:30:03,380
You walk down the aisles.

884
00:30:03,520 --> 00:30:04,280
You put the milk,

885
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:05,240
eggs, and bread

886
00:30:05,240 --> 00:30:05,840
in your cart.

887
00:30:06,260 --> 00:30:06,780
You pay.

888
00:30:07,220 --> 00:30:08,200
And as you walk out

889
00:30:08,200 --> 00:30:09,180
the automatic doors

890
00:30:09,180 --> 00:30:10,040
of the supermarket,

891
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:11,860
you take that piece of paper

892
00:30:11,860 --> 00:30:12,580
and you throw it

893
00:30:12,580 --> 00:30:13,320
in the trash can.

894
00:30:13,540 --> 00:30:14,680
The destruction of that list

895
00:30:14,680 --> 00:30:15,200
does not mean

896
00:30:15,200 --> 00:30:15,960
the note failed.

897
00:30:16,480 --> 00:30:17,800
It means the note succeeded

898
00:30:17,800 --> 00:30:19,140
with absolute perfection.

899
00:30:19,400 --> 00:30:20,660
Its continuation volume,

900
00:30:20,920 --> 00:30:22,220
the future it was pointing toward,

901
00:30:22,460 --> 00:30:23,180
dropped to zero

902
00:30:23,180 --> 00:30:23,960
because that future

903
00:30:23,960 --> 00:30:24,580
was realized.

904
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:26,260
It exhausted its purpose.

905
00:30:26,260 --> 00:30:27,660
So the ephemerality inversion

906
00:30:27,660 --> 00:30:29,020
states that the ancient notes

907
00:30:29,020 --> 00:30:30,300
we celebrate in museums,

908
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:31,980
the detailed blueprints

909
00:30:31,980 --> 00:30:32,720
for castles

910
00:30:32,720 --> 00:30:33,800
that were never built,

911
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:36,180
the agonizing drafts

912
00:30:36,180 --> 00:30:36,880
of novels

913
00:30:36,880 --> 00:30:37,980
that were never published,

914
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:39,320
the desperate letters

915
00:30:39,320 --> 00:30:40,060
that never reached

916
00:30:40,060 --> 00:30:40,660
their recipient.

917
00:30:40,900 --> 00:30:42,440
They only survived physically

918
00:30:42,440 --> 00:30:44,060
because their intended futures

919
00:30:44,060 --> 00:30:45,120
were never realized.

920
00:30:45,460 --> 00:30:45,840
Wow.

921
00:30:46,020 --> 00:30:47,260
They are, in fact,

922
00:30:47,420 --> 00:30:49,220
monuments to trajectory failure.

923
00:30:49,540 --> 00:30:50,640
They are physical evidence

924
00:30:50,640 --> 00:30:52,080
of unrealized potential.

925
00:30:52,500 --> 00:30:53,880
The truly successful notes

926
00:30:53,880 --> 00:30:54,480
of antiquity,

927
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:56,160
the ones that perfectly facilitated

928
00:30:56,160 --> 00:30:57,540
their intended continuations,

929
00:30:58,100 --> 00:30:59,840
left absolutely no trace

930
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:01,220
in the archaeological record.

931
00:31:01,320 --> 00:31:01,560
Right.

932
00:31:01,580 --> 00:31:02,620
Because they were executed,

933
00:31:03,040 --> 00:31:04,360
their futures were realized,

934
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:05,400
and they were discarded

935
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:06,920
as the creators moved forward.

936
00:31:07,080 --> 00:31:08,580
That reframes every museum

937
00:31:08,580 --> 00:31:10,100
I have ever walked into.

938
00:31:10,360 --> 00:31:11,320
The exhibits are just

939
00:31:11,320 --> 00:31:11,900
a graveyard

940
00:31:11,900 --> 00:31:13,060
of interrupted futures.

941
00:31:13,060 --> 00:31:14,640
It is a sobering lens

942
00:31:14,640 --> 00:31:15,580
through which to view

943
00:31:15,580 --> 00:31:16,500
human artifacts.

944
00:31:17,280 --> 00:31:18,060
Let's pivot back

945
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:19,580
to the machine side

946
00:31:19,580 --> 00:31:20,320
of this architecture,

947
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:21,840
moving into section 5

948
00:31:21,840 --> 00:31:22,460
of the outline,

949
00:31:22,980 --> 00:31:23,860
operational semantics

950
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:25,060
and the observation limit.

951
00:31:25,160 --> 00:31:25,740
Okay, let's get back

952
00:31:25,740 --> 00:31:26,200
to the computer.

953
00:31:26,500 --> 00:31:27,740
Because we have this beautiful,

954
00:31:27,960 --> 00:31:28,920
pristine theory

955
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:30,280
of an ever-growing history

956
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:30,920
of operators.

957
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:32,580
But as a programmer,

958
00:31:33,160 --> 00:31:35,220
I have to push back hard here.

959
00:31:35,300 --> 00:31:36,360
I was waiting for this.

960
00:31:36,580 --> 00:31:37,760
If SpherePop claims

961
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:39,440
to fundamentally abolish state,

962
00:31:39,440 --> 00:31:40,820
but ultimately

963
00:31:40,820 --> 00:31:41,760
it has to output

964
00:31:41,760 --> 00:31:42,760
a collapsed value

965
00:31:42,760 --> 00:31:43,520
for me to look at

966
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:44,000
on a monitor,

967
00:31:44,320 --> 00:31:45,560
isn't that deeply hypocritical?

968
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:46,180
Go on.

969
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:47,680
If I write a SpherePop program

970
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:48,840
to add 2 and 2

971
00:31:48,840 --> 00:31:50,100
and the screen prints

972
00:31:50,100 --> 00:31:50,780
the number 4,

973
00:31:50,960 --> 00:31:52,100
isn't 4 just a state?

974
00:31:52,580 --> 00:31:53,240
Aren't we just hiding

975
00:31:53,240 --> 00:31:54,940
the exact same state illusion

976
00:31:54,940 --> 00:31:56,040
behind a curtain

977
00:31:56,040 --> 00:31:57,260
of complex math?

978
00:31:57,540 --> 00:31:58,720
Plexian anticipated

979
00:31:58,720 --> 00:32:00,080
this exact pushback.

980
00:32:00,340 --> 00:32:00,940
In the text,

981
00:32:01,120 --> 00:32:02,560
it is explicitly referred to

982
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:04,280
as the reviewer's challenge.

983
00:32:04,560 --> 00:32:05,420
So I'm not the only one.

984
00:32:05,620 --> 00:32:06,140
Not at all.

985
00:32:06,560 --> 00:32:07,480
It is the most obvious

986
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:08,640
and necessary critique

987
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:09,420
of the entire page.

988
00:32:09,440 --> 00:32:11,760
If the output looks like state,

989
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:12,580
acts like state,

990
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:14,120
and is consumed like state,

991
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:15,100
isn't it just state?

992
00:32:15,240 --> 00:32:15,480
Right.

993
00:32:15,580 --> 00:32:16,980
You can't claim to abolish something

994
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:18,040
and then hand it to me

995
00:32:18,040 --> 00:32:19,420
as the final product.

996
00:32:19,540 --> 00:32:20,400
But the author answers

997
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:21,060
this challenge

998
00:32:21,060 --> 00:32:22,820
with theorem 17.2,

999
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:24,860
the no direct observation theorem.

1000
00:32:25,740 --> 00:32:26,560
This theorem proves

1001
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:27,400
that any system

1002
00:32:27,400 --> 00:32:28,640
that must output information

1003
00:32:28,640 --> 00:32:30,560
into a finite observable space

1004
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:32,600
must generate a state illusion.

1005
00:32:33,140 --> 00:32:34,500
It's not a bad engineering choice.

1006
00:32:34,920 --> 00:32:35,280
No.

1007
00:32:35,420 --> 00:32:36,660
It is an inescapable

1008
00:32:36,660 --> 00:32:38,460
mathematical cardinality constraint.

1009
00:32:38,460 --> 00:32:40,320
Break down the cardinality constraint for me.

1010
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:41,060
What does that mean

1011
00:32:41,060 --> 00:32:41,820
in plain English?

1012
00:32:42,140 --> 00:32:42,740
It comes down

1013
00:32:42,740 --> 00:32:43,920
to the pigeonhole principle

1014
00:32:43,920 --> 00:32:44,660
in mathematics.

1015
00:32:44,980 --> 00:32:46,020
If you have 10 pigeons

1016
00:32:46,020 --> 00:32:47,000
and 9 holes,

1017
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:48,120
at least 2 pigeons

1018
00:32:48,120 --> 00:32:48,980
must share a hole.

1019
00:32:49,240 --> 00:32:49,540
Okay.

1020
00:32:49,820 --> 00:32:50,240
Simple enough.

1021
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:52,500
The space of all possible histories

1022
00:32:52,500 --> 00:32:53,820
in a sphere pot program

1023
00:32:53,820 --> 00:32:55,400
is infinitely large.

1024
00:32:56,040 --> 00:32:57,560
Every microscopic operation

1025
00:32:57,560 --> 00:32:58,520
branches the tree

1026
00:32:58,520 --> 00:32:59,440
of history further.

1027
00:32:59,880 --> 00:33:00,700
But the space

1028
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:01,720
on your computer screen,

1029
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:02,600
the grid of pixels,

1030
00:33:02,860 --> 00:33:04,080
the finite memory buffer

1031
00:33:04,080 --> 00:33:05,660
allocated to display a result

1032
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:07,020
is strictly limited.

1033
00:33:07,260 --> 00:33:08,460
So you simply cannot map

1034
00:33:08,460 --> 00:33:09,380
an infinite number

1035
00:33:09,380 --> 00:33:10,340
of unique histories

1036
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:11,980
onto a finite number

1037
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:12,860
of display pixels

1038
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:13,940
without mapping

1039
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:15,140
multiple different histories

1040
00:33:15,140 --> 00:33:17,520
to the exact same visual output.

1041
00:33:17,640 --> 00:33:17,960
Exactly.

1042
00:33:18,100 --> 00:33:19,040
So if history A

1043
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:20,440
adds 2 plus 2

1044
00:33:20,440 --> 00:33:21,160
to get 4

1045
00:33:21,160 --> 00:33:22,440
and history B

1046
00:33:22,440 --> 00:33:23,700
subtracts 1 from 5

1047
00:33:23,700 --> 00:33:24,380
to get 4,

1048
00:33:24,560 --> 00:33:25,640
your finite screen

1049
00:33:25,640 --> 00:33:26,960
just shows the pixel pattern

1050
00:33:26,960 --> 00:33:27,480
for 4.

1051
00:33:27,920 --> 00:33:29,840
It physically cannot display

1052
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:30,460
the infinite,

1053
00:33:30,860 --> 00:33:31,840
invisible nuance

1054
00:33:31,840 --> 00:33:32,820
of the trajectory

1055
00:33:32,820 --> 00:33:33,760
that arrived at 4.

1056
00:33:33,760 --> 00:33:34,880
The output medium

1057
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:35,920
forces a collapse.

1058
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:36,540
Therefore,

1059
00:33:36,860 --> 00:33:38,000
the crucial distinction

1060
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:38,980
Flyxion introduces

1061
00:33:38,980 --> 00:33:39,720
is the difference

1062
00:33:39,720 --> 00:33:40,620
between state erasure

1063
00:33:40,620 --> 00:33:41,860
and state illusion.

1064
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:42,620
Yes.

1065
00:33:42,920 --> 00:33:43,700
And this is key.

1066
00:33:44,200 --> 00:33:45,860
Traditional computer architectures

1067
00:33:45,860 --> 00:33:46,940
like the von Neumann

1068
00:33:46,940 --> 00:33:48,160
architecture we use today

1069
00:33:48,160 --> 00:33:49,900
suffer from state erasure.

1070
00:33:50,280 --> 00:33:51,200
They compute the 4,

1071
00:33:51,400 --> 00:33:52,380
they write it to memory,

1072
00:33:52,600 --> 00:33:53,660
and they permanently

1073
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:54,820
physically delete

1074
00:33:54,820 --> 00:33:56,500
the history of the 2 plus 2

1075
00:33:56,500 --> 00:33:57,700
or the 5 minus 1.

1076
00:33:57,840 --> 00:33:58,760
But sphere pop

1077
00:33:58,760 --> 00:34:00,840
eliminates state erasure.

1078
00:34:01,220 --> 00:34:02,280
The underlying history

1079
00:34:02,280 --> 00:34:03,680
is always perfectly preserved

1080
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:05,180
and mathematically accessible

1081
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:06,300
within the deep system.

1082
00:34:06,780 --> 00:34:07,860
But sphere pop cannot

1083
00:34:07,860 --> 00:34:09,700
eliminate the state illusion

1084
00:34:09,700 --> 00:34:11,280
because for a human being

1085
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:12,640
to actually look at the result,

1086
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:14,020
we still have to look through

1087
00:34:14,020 --> 00:34:15,700
a finite, degenerate lens.

1088
00:34:16,300 --> 00:34:17,540
We are forced to use

1089
00:34:17,540 --> 00:34:18,600
the collapse operator.

1090
00:34:18,760 --> 00:34:19,880
So an observable state

1091
00:34:19,880 --> 00:34:20,980
isn't a physical thing

1092
00:34:20,980 --> 00:34:21,880
sitting on a hard drive.

1093
00:34:22,260 --> 00:34:23,980
State is just an equivalence class.

1094
00:34:24,220 --> 00:34:25,260
It's a temporary filter

1095
00:34:25,260 --> 00:34:26,560
applied over the history.

1096
00:34:26,880 --> 00:34:27,960
And the source details

1097
00:34:27,960 --> 00:34:29,580
four canonical collapse rules,

1098
00:34:29,780 --> 00:34:30,640
four different lenses

1099
00:34:30,640 --> 00:34:31,380
that you can choose

1100
00:34:31,380 --> 00:34:31,920
to look through.

1101
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:32,820
The system allows

1102
00:34:32,820 --> 00:34:33,320
a programmer

1103
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:34,560
to explicitly choose

1104
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:35,240
how the history

1105
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:35,900
has collapsed

1106
00:34:35,900 --> 00:34:36,780
for observation.

1107
00:34:37,580 --> 00:34:38,640
The four primary lenses

1108
00:34:38,640 --> 00:34:39,480
are identity,

1109
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:40,500
last write,

1110
00:34:40,900 --> 00:34:41,300
accumulate,

1111
00:34:41,740 --> 00:34:42,340
and projection.

1112
00:34:42,740 --> 00:34:43,620
Let's ground these.

1113
00:34:44,160 --> 00:34:44,820
Imagine I have

1114
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:46,020
a bank account ledger.

1115
00:34:46,260 --> 00:34:47,060
I deposit $10,

1116
00:34:47,520 --> 00:34:48,400
I withdraw $5,

1117
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:49,620
I deposit $20.

1118
00:34:50,440 --> 00:34:52,020
How do these four lenses

1119
00:34:52,020 --> 00:34:53,280
change what I see?

1120
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:54,140
Let's start

1121
00:34:54,140 --> 00:34:55,100
with the identity rule.

1122
00:34:55,740 --> 00:34:56,740
It is the finest,

1123
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:58,160
most transparent lens.

1124
00:34:58,640 --> 00:34:59,420
It simply says,

1125
00:34:59,740 --> 00:35:00,780
show me everything.

1126
00:35:00,780 --> 00:35:01,800
If you apply

1127
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:02,820
the identity collapse

1128
00:35:02,820 --> 00:35:03,740
to your bank account,

1129
00:35:04,180 --> 00:35:05,040
the output is

1130
00:35:05,040 --> 00:35:05,540
the raw,

1131
00:35:05,700 --> 00:35:06,800
unadulterated history.

1132
00:35:07,300 --> 00:35:08,400
The exact sequence

1133
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:09,720
of the $10 deposit,

1134
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:11,180
the $5 withdrawal,

1135
00:35:11,400 --> 00:35:12,740
and the $20 deposit,

1136
00:35:13,100 --> 00:35:14,840
with all their timestamps

1137
00:35:14,840 --> 00:35:16,560
and cryptographic proofs attached.

1138
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:17,960
Which is great for an audit,

1139
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:19,940
but probably way too overwhelming

1140
00:35:19,940 --> 00:35:20,680
for just checking

1141
00:35:20,680 --> 00:35:21,780
my balance at an ATM.

1142
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:22,920
Exactly the problem.

1143
00:35:23,300 --> 00:35:24,080
The second lens

1144
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:25,120
is the last write rule.

1145
00:35:25,560 --> 00:35:27,060
This mimics the traditional state

1146
00:35:27,060 --> 00:35:27,760
we are used to

1147
00:35:27,760 --> 00:35:28,900
in everyday programming.

1148
00:35:28,900 --> 00:35:30,660
It forcefully collapses

1149
00:35:30,660 --> 00:35:31,420
the history down

1150
00:35:31,420 --> 00:35:32,440
to only the most recent

1151
00:35:32,440 --> 00:35:33,000
pop event

1152
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:34,000
for a given symbol.

1153
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:35,740
So if a variable changed

1154
00:35:35,740 --> 00:35:37,160
from 1 to 2 to 3,

1155
00:35:37,580 --> 00:35:38,740
the last write lens

1156
00:35:38,740 --> 00:35:39,920
only shows you the 3.

1157
00:35:40,280 --> 00:35:41,740
It hides the journey entirely.

1158
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:42,920
But the crucial difference

1159
00:35:42,920 --> 00:35:43,640
is that the journey

1160
00:35:43,640 --> 00:35:45,060
is still safely hidden underneath,

1161
00:35:45,400 --> 00:35:45,620
right?

1162
00:35:45,900 --> 00:35:47,180
The system hasn't deleted

1163
00:35:47,180 --> 00:35:48,040
the 1 and the 2,

1164
00:35:48,120 --> 00:35:48,720
you've just chosen

1165
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:49,500
to filter them

1166
00:35:49,500 --> 00:35:50,420
out of your current view.

1167
00:35:50,660 --> 00:35:52,080
That is the defining distinction.

1168
00:35:52,580 --> 00:35:53,560
The third lens

1169
00:35:53,560 --> 00:35:54,740
is the accumulate rule.

1170
00:35:55,220 --> 00:35:56,280
This maps a symbol

1171
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:57,880
to a mathematical reduction

1172
00:35:57,880 --> 00:35:58,860
of its events.

1173
00:35:59,100 --> 00:36:00,340
In your bank account example,

1174
00:36:00,560 --> 00:36:01,560
the accumulate lens

1175
00:36:01,560 --> 00:36:02,720
would take the plus 10,

1176
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:03,580
the minus 5,

1177
00:36:03,600 --> 00:36:04,360
and the plus 20,

1178
00:36:04,760 --> 00:36:06,040
run a summation function,

1179
00:36:06,500 --> 00:36:07,860
and output the final balance

1180
00:36:07,860 --> 00:36:08,440
of $25.

1181
00:36:08,880 --> 00:36:09,760
And the fourth lens,

1182
00:36:10,180 --> 00:36:11,080
the projection rule.

1183
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:12,200
The projection lens

1184
00:36:12,200 --> 00:36:13,200
filters the history

1185
00:36:13,200 --> 00:36:14,700
based on specific metadata

1186
00:36:14,700 --> 00:36:15,600
or tags.

1187
00:36:16,060 --> 00:36:17,120
It might only show events

1188
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:17,580
that match

1189
00:36:17,580 --> 00:36:19,200
a specific security clearance

1190
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:20,640
or events generated

1191
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:21,800
by a specific user.

1192
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:23,040
The grand takeaway here

1193
00:36:23,040 --> 00:36:23,700
is that state

1194
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:25,360
is never a fundamental reality.

1195
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:27,220
State depends entirely

1196
00:36:27,220 --> 00:36:27,900
on which of these

1197
00:36:27,900 --> 00:36:28,820
mathematical lenses

1198
00:36:28,820 --> 00:36:30,220
you choose to bolt

1199
00:36:30,220 --> 00:36:30,880
onto the front

1200
00:36:30,880 --> 00:36:31,500
of your history.

1201
00:36:31,740 --> 00:36:32,960
Beautifully summarized.

1202
00:36:33,100 --> 00:36:34,140
Okay, this brings up

1203
00:36:34,140 --> 00:36:35,440
a massive structural question

1204
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:36,620
moving into section 6

1205
00:36:36,620 --> 00:36:37,280
on type theory.

1206
00:36:37,860 --> 00:36:39,340
If state is just a lens

1207
00:36:39,340 --> 00:36:40,440
and underneath it

1208
00:36:40,440 --> 00:36:41,800
is this massive chaotic

1209
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:42,700
ocean of history,

1210
00:36:43,320 --> 00:36:44,200
how do we ensure

1211
00:36:44,200 --> 00:36:45,040
that these collapses

1212
00:36:45,040 --> 00:36:46,040
don't just output

1213
00:36:46,040 --> 00:36:46,920
total garbage?

1214
00:36:47,260 --> 00:36:48,160
To maintain order,

1215
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:49,320
the system needs rules.

1216
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:51,580
But types in SpherePop,

1217
00:36:51,580 --> 00:36:52,360
the way the system

1218
00:36:52,360 --> 00:36:53,340
categorizes data

1219
00:36:53,340 --> 00:36:54,920
are fundamentally different

1220
00:36:54,920 --> 00:36:56,160
from types in normal languages

1221
00:36:56,160 --> 00:36:57,560
like Python or Java.

1222
00:36:58,040 --> 00:36:58,920
In traditional languages,

1223
00:36:59,120 --> 00:37:00,620
a type like an integer,

1224
00:37:01,120 --> 00:37:02,460
a boolean, or a string

1225
00:37:02,460 --> 00:37:03,700
is just a definition

1226
00:37:03,700 --> 00:37:05,640
of a set of admissible values.

1227
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:07,860
If you declare a variable

1228
00:37:07,860 --> 00:37:08,540
as an integer,

1229
00:37:09,140 --> 00:37:10,220
the compiler ensures

1230
00:37:10,220 --> 00:37:11,500
it holds a 1

1231
00:37:11,500 --> 00:37:12,960
or a 5 or 100

1232
00:37:12,960 --> 00:37:14,700
but not the word apple.

1233
00:37:14,820 --> 00:37:16,400
That is a value set type system.

1234
00:37:16,500 --> 00:37:17,640
But in a history primary

1235
00:37:17,640 --> 00:37:19,240
architecture like SpherePop,

1236
00:37:19,240 --> 00:37:21,100
merely verifying

1237
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:22,140
the final value

1238
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:23,600
is completely insufficient.

1239
00:37:24,300 --> 00:37:25,060
Why is it insufficient?

1240
00:37:25,220 --> 00:37:26,060
I mean, if it's a number,

1241
00:37:26,240 --> 00:37:26,700
it's a number.

1242
00:37:26,820 --> 00:37:28,600
Because a value in SpherePop

1243
00:37:28,600 --> 00:37:30,360
does not exist in a vacuum.

1244
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:31,940
It is the terminal point

1245
00:37:31,940 --> 00:37:33,540
of a specific path

1246
00:37:33,540 --> 00:37:34,300
through history.

1247
00:37:34,900 --> 00:37:36,260
So a type in SpherePop

1248
00:37:36,260 --> 00:37:37,460
is not just a label,

1249
00:37:37,780 --> 00:37:39,820
it is an admissibility certificate.

1250
00:37:40,060 --> 00:37:40,980
An admissibility certificate?

1251
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:42,200
A value of type T

1252
00:37:42,200 --> 00:37:45,260
is a formal cryptographic proof

1253
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:46,720
that the historical path

1254
00:37:46,720 --> 00:37:47,580
taken to arrive

1255
00:37:47,580 --> 00:37:48,840
at this specific value

1256
00:37:48,840 --> 00:37:50,040
did not violate

1257
00:37:50,040 --> 00:37:51,280
any system constraints

1258
00:37:51,280 --> 00:37:51,940
along the way.

1259
00:37:52,240 --> 00:37:53,300
The type certifies

1260
00:37:53,300 --> 00:37:54,740
the legitimacy of the journey,

1261
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:55,880
not just the shape

1262
00:37:55,880 --> 00:37:56,700
of the destination.

1263
00:37:56,940 --> 00:37:57,440
Let me see if I can

1264
00:37:57,440 --> 00:37:58,000
translate that.

1265
00:37:58,280 --> 00:37:58,900
If I have a variable

1266
00:37:58,900 --> 00:37:59,860
in a traditional program

1267
00:37:59,860 --> 00:38:01,320
that represents a password file,

1268
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:02,140
the type just says

1269
00:38:02,140 --> 00:38:03,260
this is a string of text.

1270
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:04,740
But in SpherePop,

1271
00:38:05,220 --> 00:38:06,340
the type doesn't just say

1272
00:38:06,340 --> 00:38:07,220
this is text.

1273
00:38:07,380 --> 00:38:09,220
The type is a historical passport.

1274
00:38:09,780 --> 00:38:11,220
It says this is text

1275
00:38:11,220 --> 00:38:13,020
and here is the mathematical proof

1276
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:14,900
that you had administrator permissions

1277
00:38:14,900 --> 00:38:15,900
when you asked for it.

1278
00:38:15,900 --> 00:38:16,800
Here's the proof

1279
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:17,960
the file actually existed

1280
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:18,640
on the drive

1281
00:38:18,640 --> 00:38:19,780
and here's the proof

1282
00:38:19,780 --> 00:38:20,760
that the read operation

1283
00:38:20,760 --> 00:38:22,180
completed without corruption.

1284
00:38:22,920 --> 00:38:24,440
That is a brilliant translation.

1285
00:38:25,200 --> 00:38:26,200
The type is a passport

1286
00:38:26,200 --> 00:38:27,920
covered in verified stamps.

1287
00:38:28,420 --> 00:38:29,980
And this heavy reliance

1288
00:38:29,980 --> 00:38:31,020
on historical proof

1289
00:38:31,020 --> 00:38:32,240
leads to one of the most

1290
00:38:32,240 --> 00:38:33,420
fascinating tangents

1291
00:38:33,420 --> 00:38:34,340
in the monograph.

1292
00:38:34,540 --> 00:38:35,400
Oh, about the interpreter.

1293
00:38:35,680 --> 00:38:36,120
Yes.

1294
00:38:36,580 --> 00:38:37,360
The author details

1295
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:38,480
a specific conflict

1296
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:39,140
they encountered

1297
00:38:39,140 --> 00:38:39,940
while building

1298
00:38:39,940 --> 00:38:41,060
the SpherePop interpreter,

1299
00:38:41,620 --> 00:38:42,400
a conflict between

1300
00:38:42,400 --> 00:38:43,360
the interpreter engine

1301
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:44,300
and the type checker

1302
00:38:44,300 --> 00:38:45,800
regarding free variables.

1303
00:38:46,140 --> 00:38:47,100
Yeah, I flagged that section.

1304
00:38:47,200 --> 00:38:47,700
It sounded like

1305
00:38:47,700 --> 00:38:48,980
they ran into a nasty bug

1306
00:38:48,980 --> 00:38:49,780
and ended up turning it

1307
00:38:49,780 --> 00:38:50,580
into a profound

1308
00:38:50,580 --> 00:38:51,620
philosophical feature.

1309
00:38:52,080 --> 00:38:53,380
What exactly was the conflict?

1310
00:38:53,680 --> 00:38:54,320
It comes down

1311
00:38:54,320 --> 00:38:56,480
to a deep epistemological choice

1312
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:57,340
regarding open

1313
00:38:57,340 --> 00:38:58,540
versus closed worlds.

1314
00:38:58,940 --> 00:38:59,940
When the type checker

1315
00:38:59,940 --> 00:39:00,940
is scanning the code

1316
00:39:00,940 --> 00:39:01,860
and it encounters

1317
00:39:01,860 --> 00:39:03,060
a variable or a symbol

1318
00:39:03,060 --> 00:39:03,820
that has never been

1319
00:39:03,820 --> 00:39:04,600
declared before,

1320
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:05,780
a free variable,

1321
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:06,700
what should it do?

1322
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:08,180
Well, in my experience,

1323
00:39:08,460 --> 00:39:09,660
the compiler immediately

1324
00:39:09,660 --> 00:39:10,620
throws a red error

1325
00:39:10,620 --> 00:39:11,340
and stops,

1326
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:13,080
variable undefined.

1327
00:39:13,420 --> 00:39:14,740
That reaction is based

1328
00:39:14,740 --> 00:39:16,220
on a closed world assumption.

1329
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:18,320
A closed world assumes

1330
00:39:18,320 --> 00:39:19,060
that the compiler

1331
00:39:19,060 --> 00:39:20,160
possesses complete,

1332
00:39:20,420 --> 00:39:21,120
total knowledge

1333
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:21,700
of the universe.

1334
00:39:22,540 --> 00:39:23,540
If the system

1335
00:39:23,540 --> 00:39:24,640
doesn't know about something,

1336
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:26,180
it concludes that the thing

1337
00:39:26,180 --> 00:39:27,980
fundamentally does not exist.

1338
00:39:28,680 --> 00:39:30,460
Therefore, referencing it

1339
00:39:30,460 --> 00:39:31,300
is an illegal,

1340
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:32,720
inadmissible act.

1341
00:39:32,980 --> 00:39:34,260
But an open world context

1342
00:39:34,260 --> 00:39:34,900
is different.

1343
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:36,440
An open world context

1344
00:39:36,440 --> 00:39:38,080
assumes partial knowledge.

1345
00:39:38,480 --> 00:39:39,620
It acknowledges that

1346
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:40,620
the system is embedded

1347
00:39:40,620 --> 00:39:41,420
in a larger,

1348
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:42,880
unpredictable universe.

1349
00:39:43,500 --> 00:39:44,940
Under an open world assumption,

1350
00:39:45,420 --> 00:39:46,680
the absence of a declaration

1351
00:39:46,680 --> 00:39:47,260
doesn't mean

1352
00:39:47,260 --> 00:39:48,160
this doesn't exist.

1353
00:39:48,540 --> 00:39:49,180
It just means

1354
00:39:49,180 --> 00:39:50,920
this is an undecided possibility.

1355
00:39:51,240 --> 00:39:51,900
Let me try to build

1356
00:39:51,900 --> 00:39:52,880
an analogy for this.

1357
00:39:53,140 --> 00:39:53,740
It's the difference

1358
00:39:53,740 --> 00:39:54,500
between ordering

1359
00:39:54,500 --> 00:39:55,420
in a strict restaurant

1360
00:39:55,420 --> 00:39:56,180
versus hosting

1361
00:39:56,180 --> 00:39:57,200
a neighborhood potluck.

1362
00:39:57,400 --> 00:39:58,280
Oh, I like this.

1363
00:39:58,420 --> 00:39:59,420
If I am at a restaurant

1364
00:39:59,420 --> 00:40:00,500
with a closed world menu

1365
00:40:00,500 --> 00:40:01,760
and I try to order a taco,

1366
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:02,620
but tacos aren't

1367
00:40:02,620 --> 00:40:03,500
on the printed menu,

1368
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:04,820
the waiter says,

1369
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:06,560
error, item, undefined.

1370
00:40:06,660 --> 00:40:07,800
You cannot order that.

1371
00:40:07,800 --> 00:40:08,800
The menu is

1372
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:09,820
the entire universe.

1373
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:11,360
But if I am hosting

1374
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:12,700
an open world potluck

1375
00:40:12,700 --> 00:40:14,400
and someone walks in

1376
00:40:14,400 --> 00:40:15,120
carrying a dish

1377
00:40:15,120 --> 00:40:16,140
I've never seen before,

1378
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:17,420
I don't throw an error

1379
00:40:17,420 --> 00:40:18,220
and kick them out.

1380
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:20,000
The absence of the dish

1381
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:21,040
from my initial plan

1382
00:40:21,040 --> 00:40:22,360
just means my knowledge

1383
00:40:22,360 --> 00:40:23,120
was incomplete.

1384
00:40:23,980 --> 00:40:25,200
I accept the new dish

1385
00:40:25,200 --> 00:40:27,060
as an undecided possibility

1386
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:27,980
entering the system.

1387
00:40:28,260 --> 00:40:28,960
That captures

1388
00:40:28,960 --> 00:40:30,220
the epistemological divide

1389
00:40:30,220 --> 00:40:30,760
perfectly.

1390
00:40:31,300 --> 00:40:31,880
Sphere Pop

1391
00:40:31,880 --> 00:40:33,020
forces the programmer

1392
00:40:33,020 --> 00:40:34,560
to explicitly declare

1393
00:40:34,560 --> 00:40:35,760
which world boundary

1394
00:40:35,760 --> 00:40:36,480
is in force.

1395
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:37,500
You have to tell

1396
00:40:37,500 --> 00:40:38,020
the compiler

1397
00:40:38,020 --> 00:40:39,020
whether it is operating

1398
00:40:39,020 --> 00:40:39,860
in a closed,

1399
00:40:40,040 --> 00:40:41,220
strictly controlled restaurant

1400
00:40:41,220 --> 00:40:42,480
or an open,

1401
00:40:42,640 --> 00:40:43,720
dynamic potluck.

1402
00:40:43,960 --> 00:40:44,680
And this fundamentally

1403
00:40:44,680 --> 00:40:46,000
ties into how the system

1404
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:47,420
handles errors natively

1405
00:40:47,420 --> 00:40:48,520
through a mechanism

1406
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:50,020
called proof-carrying refusal.

1407
00:40:50,240 --> 00:40:51,220
We talked about refusal

1408
00:40:51,220 --> 00:40:52,780
earlier as documenting

1409
00:40:52,780 --> 00:40:54,260
a tiger behind a door.

1410
00:40:54,820 --> 00:40:55,640
How does proof-carrying

1411
00:40:55,640 --> 00:40:56,440
refusal work

1412
00:40:56,440 --> 00:40:58,340
when code actually breaks?

1413
00:40:58,620 --> 00:40:59,740
In a standard program

1414
00:40:59,740 --> 00:41:01,240
when a fatal error occurs,

1415
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:02,940
say, trying to divide by zero,

1416
00:41:03,320 --> 00:41:04,560
the program either crashes

1417
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:06,480
silently or it throws an exception

1418
00:41:06,480 --> 00:41:07,720
that unwinds and destroys

1419
00:41:07,720 --> 00:41:08,540
the stacked race.

1420
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:09,560
In Sphere Pop,

1421
00:41:09,860 --> 00:41:10,740
the inadmissibility

1422
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:11,560
is carried forward

1423
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:12,260
into the future

1424
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:13,420
as a formal proof.

1425
00:41:13,940 --> 00:41:15,000
When the error occurs,

1426
00:41:15,120 --> 00:41:15,640
it is recorded

1427
00:41:15,640 --> 00:41:16,740
as a refuse event.

1428
00:41:17,200 --> 00:41:17,940
But this event

1429
00:41:17,940 --> 00:41:18,780
isn't a crash.

1430
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:19,760
The error itself

1431
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:21,320
becomes a first-class piece

1432
00:41:21,320 --> 00:41:22,740
of type-level information.

1433
00:41:23,280 --> 00:41:24,540
It carries the cryptographic

1434
00:41:24,540 --> 00:41:26,160
evidence of its own failure.

1435
00:41:26,640 --> 00:41:27,900
So the system doesn't stop.

1436
00:41:28,440 --> 00:41:29,180
It just holds up

1437
00:41:29,180 --> 00:41:30,240
a mathematical certificate

1438
00:41:30,240 --> 00:41:30,820
that says,

1439
00:41:31,260 --> 00:41:32,160
I tried to do this

1440
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:33,240
and here's exactly

1441
00:41:33,240 --> 00:41:33,900
why it broke,

1442
00:41:34,300 --> 00:41:35,320
but what if I want

1443
00:41:35,320 --> 00:41:36,080
to write code

1444
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:37,080
to fix that error?

1445
00:41:37,700 --> 00:41:38,600
Handling an error

1446
00:41:38,600 --> 00:41:39,520
in this architecture

1447
00:41:39,520 --> 00:41:40,640
requires you to generate

1448
00:41:40,640 --> 00:41:41,760
a recovery certificate.

1449
00:41:42,220 --> 00:41:43,000
It is essentially

1450
00:41:43,000 --> 00:41:44,460
the computational equivalent

1451
00:41:44,460 --> 00:41:45,640
of a repair note

1452
00:41:45,640 --> 00:41:46,360
from the taxonomy

1453
00:41:46,360 --> 00:41:47,000
we discussed.

1454
00:41:47,260 --> 00:41:48,140
A recovery certificate

1455
00:41:48,140 --> 00:41:49,480
is a mathematical proof

1456
00:41:49,480 --> 00:41:50,820
that formally transforms

1457
00:41:50,820 --> 00:41:52,860
the documented inadmissibility,

1458
00:41:53,080 --> 00:41:53,900
the error state,

1459
00:41:54,340 --> 00:41:55,120
into a valid

1460
00:41:55,120 --> 00:41:56,400
admissible continuation.

1461
00:41:57,000 --> 00:41:58,240
You aren't just using

1462
00:41:58,240 --> 00:41:59,220
a try-catch block

1463
00:41:59,220 --> 00:42:00,100
to sweep the crash

1464
00:42:00,100 --> 00:42:00,660
under the rug.

1465
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:01,440
You are appending

1466
00:42:01,440 --> 00:42:02,560
a certified patch

1467
00:42:02,560 --> 00:42:04,080
to the permanent history log

1468
00:42:04,080 --> 00:42:05,800
showing exactly what broke

1469
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:07,340
and mathematically proving

1470
00:42:07,340 --> 00:42:08,240
how your new code

1471
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:09,840
safely bypasses the failure.

1472
00:42:10,240 --> 00:42:11,840
This sounds incredibly robust,

1473
00:42:12,280 --> 00:42:12,920
but it brings up

1474
00:42:12,920 --> 00:42:13,980
an absolutely massive

1475
00:42:13,980 --> 00:42:14,960
practical problem,

1476
00:42:15,320 --> 00:42:16,020
which leads us

1477
00:42:16,020 --> 00:42:16,920
to Section 7,

1478
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:18,240
Garbage Collection

1479
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:19,580
and Compiler Correctness.

1480
00:42:19,680 --> 00:42:20,660
The elephant in the room.

1481
00:42:20,820 --> 00:42:22,240
If histories never die

1482
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:23,940
and every single

1483
00:42:23,940 --> 00:42:25,020
variable assignment,

1484
00:42:25,200 --> 00:42:26,440
every branch taken,

1485
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:27,940
every condition checked,

1486
00:42:27,940 --> 00:42:29,660
and every error encountered

1487
00:42:29,660 --> 00:42:31,280
is kept as a permanent,

1488
00:42:31,660 --> 00:42:33,340
cryptographically certified record,

1489
00:42:33,900 --> 00:42:34,140
I mean,

1490
00:42:34,200 --> 00:42:34,760
the listener's probably

1491
00:42:34,760 --> 00:42:35,620
screaming right now,

1492
00:42:35,860 --> 00:42:37,040
how does a computer

1493
00:42:37,040 --> 00:42:37,740
running this

1494
00:42:37,740 --> 00:42:39,040
not run out of RAM

1495
00:42:39,040 --> 00:42:39,820
in five seconds?

1496
00:42:39,820 --> 00:42:41,800
The memory explosion problem

1497
00:42:41,800 --> 00:42:42,740
is the crucible

1498
00:42:42,740 --> 00:42:44,740
for trajectory-based computation.

1499
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:46,780
Traditional garbage collection,

1500
00:42:47,040 --> 00:42:47,680
or GC,

1501
00:42:48,020 --> 00:42:49,200
is relatively simple.

1502
00:42:49,340 --> 00:42:50,160
The runtime engine

1503
00:42:50,160 --> 00:42:51,000
scans the memory,

1504
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:52,600
looks for data objects

1505
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:53,280
that the program

1506
00:42:53,280 --> 00:42:54,420
is no longer pointing to,

1507
00:42:54,720 --> 00:42:55,760
and simply deletes them

1508
00:42:55,760 --> 00:42:56,640
to free up space.

1509
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:57,980
Because if nothing is looking at it,

1510
00:42:58,020 --> 00:42:59,040
it doesn't exist anymore.

1511
00:42:59,240 --> 00:42:59,960
But in SpherePop,

1512
00:43:00,300 --> 00:43:01,920
GC is completely redefined

1513
00:43:01,920 --> 00:43:04,080
as admissibility-guided collapse.

1514
00:43:04,280 --> 00:43:04,480
Yeah.

1515
00:43:04,700 --> 00:43:05,640
You cannot simply

1516
00:43:05,640 --> 00:43:06,780
delete historical events

1517
00:43:06,780 --> 00:43:08,620
just because no current variable

1518
00:43:08,620 --> 00:43:09,860
is directly pointing to them.

1519
00:43:09,900 --> 00:43:11,600
Because that deep history

1520
00:43:11,600 --> 00:43:12,880
might contain

1521
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:14,300
the exact admissibility

1522
00:43:14,300 --> 00:43:15,320
certificates required

1523
00:43:15,320 --> 00:43:16,000
to prove that

1524
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:16,860
a future collapse

1525
00:43:16,860 --> 00:43:17,880
operation is valid.

1526
00:43:18,320 --> 00:43:19,180
If you delete

1527
00:43:19,180 --> 00:43:19,600
the history

1528
00:43:19,600 --> 00:43:20,700
of the passport stamps,

1529
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:22,160
you can no longer prove

1530
00:43:22,160 --> 00:43:23,180
the passport is real

1531
00:43:23,180 --> 00:43:24,020
when you hit the border.

1532
00:43:24,020 --> 00:43:25,140
Because the type checker

1533
00:43:25,140 --> 00:43:26,480
relies on the entire journey

1534
00:43:26,480 --> 00:43:27,240
to prove safety,

1535
00:43:27,380 --> 00:43:28,600
so how do you delete anything?

1536
00:43:29,180 --> 00:43:30,460
GC in SpherePop

1537
00:43:30,460 --> 00:43:31,760
is the delicate art

1538
00:43:31,760 --> 00:43:32,460
of collapsing

1539
00:43:32,460 --> 00:43:33,740
dead segments of history

1540
00:43:33,740 --> 00:43:34,540
to save space,

1541
00:43:34,740 --> 00:43:36,360
while rigidly preserving

1542
00:43:36,360 --> 00:43:37,500
the structural certificates

1543
00:43:37,500 --> 00:43:38,720
required to satisfy

1544
00:43:38,720 --> 00:43:40,100
future type checks.

1545
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:41,860
The monograph outlines

1546
00:43:41,860 --> 00:43:43,720
three specific GC strategies

1547
00:43:43,720 --> 00:43:44,520
that trade off

1548
00:43:44,520 --> 00:43:45,580
between memory efficiency

1549
00:43:45,580 --> 00:43:47,200
and historical fidelity.

1550
00:43:47,580 --> 00:43:48,380
The three are

1551
00:43:48,380 --> 00:43:48,900
MarkScan,

1552
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:49,380
Quotient,

1553
00:43:49,440 --> 00:43:50,400
and Certificate only.

1554
00:43:50,800 --> 00:43:51,780
Break these down for us.

1555
00:43:52,000 --> 00:43:53,560
MarkScan is the most conservative,

1556
00:43:53,560 --> 00:43:54,720
and the most expensive.

1557
00:43:55,140 --> 00:43:56,100
It traverses the entire

1558
00:43:56,100 --> 00:43:56,760
history tree

1559
00:43:56,760 --> 00:43:57,920
and preserves absolutely

1560
00:43:57,920 --> 00:43:58,840
everything that is

1561
00:43:58,840 --> 00:43:59,720
mathematically linked

1562
00:43:59,720 --> 00:44:00,660
to a potential future.

1563
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:02,300
It maintains perfect fidelity,

1564
00:44:02,640 --> 00:44:03,220
but it consumes

1565
00:44:03,220 --> 00:44:04,340
vast amounts of memory.

1566
00:44:04,540 --> 00:44:05,340
And Quotient GC.

1567
00:44:05,820 --> 00:44:06,540
Quotient GC,

1568
00:44:06,680 --> 00:44:07,220
on the other hand,

1569
00:44:07,240 --> 00:44:07,840
is the closest

1570
00:44:07,840 --> 00:44:08,780
to what we do today.

1571
00:44:09,340 --> 00:44:10,640
It forcefully collapses

1572
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:11,600
sections of history

1573
00:44:11,600 --> 00:44:12,340
down to just

1574
00:44:12,340 --> 00:44:13,620
their last known state.

1575
00:44:14,100 --> 00:44:15,720
It saves massive amounts

1576
00:44:15,720 --> 00:44:16,160
of memory,

1577
00:44:16,520 --> 00:44:17,280
but it permanently

1578
00:44:17,280 --> 00:44:18,420
destroys your ability

1579
00:44:18,420 --> 00:44:19,720
to rewind and replay

1580
00:44:19,720 --> 00:44:21,380
that specific section

1581
00:44:21,380 --> 00:44:22,100
of the trajectory.

1582
00:44:22,100 --> 00:44:23,660
So Quotient GC

1583
00:44:23,660 --> 00:44:24,920
is admitting defeat

1584
00:44:24,920 --> 00:44:25,660
and returning

1585
00:44:25,660 --> 00:44:26,580
to the state illusion

1586
00:44:26,580 --> 00:44:27,560
to save RAM.

1587
00:44:28,040 --> 00:44:29,000
What about the third one,

1588
00:44:29,220 --> 00:44:29,960
Certificate Only?

1589
00:44:30,520 --> 00:44:31,760
Certificate Only GC

1590
00:44:31,760 --> 00:44:32,860
is the uniquely

1591
00:44:32,860 --> 00:44:34,320
sphere-pop approach.

1592
00:44:34,880 --> 00:44:35,980
It analyzes

1593
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:37,180
a historical trajectory

1594
00:44:37,180 --> 00:44:38,380
and deletes

1595
00:44:38,380 --> 00:44:39,940
the actual raw values

1596
00:44:39,940 --> 00:44:40,920
and the pop events,

1597
00:44:41,100 --> 00:44:42,420
the heavy data payloads,

1598
00:44:42,460 --> 00:44:43,320
to save memory.

1599
00:44:43,720 --> 00:44:45,060
But it rigidly extracts

1600
00:44:45,060 --> 00:44:46,700
and keeps all the refuse proofs

1601
00:44:46,700 --> 00:44:47,500
and the type certificates.

1602
00:44:47,860 --> 00:44:48,560
It throws away

1603
00:44:48,560 --> 00:44:49,480
the beta you don't need,

1604
00:44:49,560 --> 00:44:49,920
but it keeps

1605
00:44:49,920 --> 00:44:51,000
the mathematical skeleton

1606
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:52,100
that guarantees the data

1607
00:44:52,100 --> 00:44:53,220
was processed legally.

1608
00:44:53,500 --> 00:44:54,260
That is fascinating.

1609
00:44:54,380 --> 00:44:55,060
It's like throwing away

1610
00:44:55,060 --> 00:44:55,800
the heavy textbook

1611
00:44:55,800 --> 00:44:56,960
but keeping the index

1612
00:44:56,960 --> 00:44:58,020
in the table of contents

1613
00:44:58,020 --> 00:44:59,180
so you still know

1614
00:44:59,180 --> 00:44:59,680
the structure

1615
00:44:59,680 --> 00:45:00,380
of what was there.

1616
00:45:00,500 --> 00:45:01,660
And this intense focus

1617
00:45:01,660 --> 00:45:02,400
on preserving

1618
00:45:02,400 --> 00:45:03,600
the structure of history

1619
00:45:03,600 --> 00:45:05,040
completely changes

1620
00:45:05,040 --> 00:45:05,700
the definition

1621
00:45:05,700 --> 00:45:06,860
of a correct compiler.

1622
00:45:07,340 --> 00:45:08,000
The source has

1623
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:08,680
a whole section

1624
00:45:08,680 --> 00:45:10,440
contrasting compiler correctness

1625
00:45:10,440 --> 00:45:11,500
in traditional languages

1626
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:12,920
versus sphere-pop.

1627
00:45:13,200 --> 00:45:13,560
Yes.

1628
00:45:14,080 --> 00:45:15,420
In traditional compilation,

1629
00:45:15,900 --> 00:45:16,680
the goal standard

1630
00:45:16,680 --> 00:45:18,280
is output equivalence.

1631
00:45:18,280 --> 00:45:20,640
If you write a program in C

1632
00:45:20,640 --> 00:45:21,480
that calculates

1633
00:45:21,480 --> 00:45:22,540
a complex algorithm

1634
00:45:22,540 --> 00:45:24,020
and outputs the number 42

1635
00:45:24,020 --> 00:45:25,240
and you run it

1636
00:45:25,240 --> 00:45:25,920
through a compiler,

1637
00:45:26,380 --> 00:45:27,400
the compiler's job

1638
00:45:27,400 --> 00:45:28,760
is to generate machine code.

1639
00:45:29,280 --> 00:45:30,620
As long as that machine code

1640
00:45:30,620 --> 00:45:32,280
also outputs the number 42

1641
00:45:32,280 --> 00:45:33,540
under the same conditions,

1642
00:45:34,080 --> 00:45:35,260
the compiler is considered

1643
00:45:35,260 --> 00:45:36,440
mathematically correct.

1644
00:45:36,860 --> 00:45:37,820
It doesn't matter

1645
00:45:37,820 --> 00:45:38,480
if the compiler

1646
00:45:38,480 --> 00:45:39,600
rearranged the loops,

1647
00:45:39,920 --> 00:45:41,200
deleted unused variables,

1648
00:45:41,480 --> 00:45:42,500
or took a completely

1649
00:45:42,500 --> 00:45:43,220
different path

1650
00:45:43,220 --> 00:45:43,800
under the hood

1651
00:45:43,800 --> 00:45:44,860
to optimize speed.

1652
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:46,240
As long as the destination

1653
00:45:46,240 --> 00:45:47,040
is the same,

1654
00:45:47,320 --> 00:45:48,140
it is correct.

1655
00:45:48,280 --> 00:45:49,460
But in sphere-pop,

1656
00:45:49,840 --> 00:45:50,360
the compiler

1657
00:45:50,360 --> 00:45:51,100
doesn't output

1658
00:45:51,100 --> 00:45:52,140
raw machine code.

1659
00:45:52,460 --> 00:45:53,300
It outputs an event

1660
00:45:53,300 --> 00:45:54,600
intermediate representation,

1661
00:45:55,060 --> 00:45:55,700
an event IR,

1662
00:45:56,440 --> 00:45:57,200
and the author states

1663
00:45:57,200 --> 00:45:58,040
it must achieve

1664
00:45:58,040 --> 00:45:59,180
history equivalence.

1665
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:00,580
This is a radical shift.

1666
00:46:00,820 --> 00:46:01,340
In sphere-pop,

1667
00:46:01,500 --> 00:46:02,100
two programs

1668
00:46:02,100 --> 00:46:02,520
that arrive

1669
00:46:02,520 --> 00:46:03,900
at the exact same answer

1670
00:46:03,900 --> 00:46:04,620
are fundamentally

1671
00:46:04,620 --> 00:46:05,380
not equivalent

1672
00:46:05,380 --> 00:46:06,040
if they took

1673
00:46:06,040 --> 00:46:07,240
different historical paths

1674
00:46:07,240 --> 00:46:07,780
to get there.

1675
00:46:08,060 --> 00:46:09,300
Let's really emphasize this.

1676
00:46:09,640 --> 00:46:10,560
If I write code

1677
00:46:10,560 --> 00:46:11,480
that takes path A

1678
00:46:11,480 --> 00:46:12,840
to output the number 5,

1679
00:46:13,200 --> 00:46:13,880
and the compiler

1680
00:46:13,880 --> 00:46:14,860
thinks it's being clever

1681
00:46:14,860 --> 00:46:16,520
and optimizes the execution

1682
00:46:16,520 --> 00:46:17,860
to take a faster path B

1683
00:46:17,860 --> 00:46:19,100
to output the number 5,

1684
00:46:19,460 --> 00:46:20,420
the compiler is broken.

1685
00:46:20,740 --> 00:46:21,840
The compiler is broken

1686
00:46:21,840 --> 00:46:23,280
because it altered the history.

1687
00:46:24,040 --> 00:46:24,380
Remember,

1688
00:46:24,820 --> 00:46:25,480
in sphere-pop,

1689
00:46:25,720 --> 00:46:27,300
types are historical certificates.

1690
00:46:27,960 --> 00:46:29,320
If the compiler silently

1691
00:46:29,320 --> 00:46:30,380
changes path A

1692
00:46:30,380 --> 00:46:31,120
to path B,

1693
00:46:31,580 --> 00:46:32,540
the compiled code

1694
00:46:32,540 --> 00:46:33,040
might output

1695
00:46:33,040 --> 00:46:34,260
the correct number today,

1696
00:46:34,520 --> 00:46:36,200
but it might fatally fail

1697
00:46:36,200 --> 00:46:37,380
a type check tomorrow

1698
00:46:37,380 --> 00:46:38,720
when a future operation

1699
00:46:38,720 --> 00:46:40,260
relies on the specific,

1700
00:46:40,720 --> 00:46:41,560
certified events

1701
00:46:41,560 --> 00:46:42,140
that were supposed

1702
00:46:42,140 --> 00:46:43,400
to happen on path A.

1703
00:46:43,400 --> 00:46:44,160
The path

1704
00:46:44,160 --> 00:46:45,060
is the program.

1705
00:46:45,880 --> 00:46:46,700
Output equivalence

1706
00:46:46,700 --> 00:46:47,460
is a strictly

1707
00:46:47,460 --> 00:46:48,620
weaker criterion

1708
00:46:48,620 --> 00:46:49,820
that is completely

1709
00:46:49,820 --> 00:46:51,080
insufficient for a framework

1710
00:46:51,080 --> 00:46:51,760
where history

1711
00:46:51,760 --> 00:46:53,220
is the primary substance.

1712
